addRun("a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer--writer-claude-haiku-5-5--harvey-labs--codex--real-estate-identify-issues-in-counterpartys-proposed-commercial-real-estate-loan-agreement", {"meta": {"benchmark": "harvey-labs", "harness": "codex", "model": "qwen3.8-27b", "task_title": "Identify Issues in Counterparty's Proposed Commercial Real Estate Loan Agreement — Issue Memorandum", "axis": "minute", "set": "a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer", "arm": "writer-claude-haiku-5-5"}, "events": [{"id": "event_001", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_002", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_003", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_004", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_005", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_006", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_007", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_008", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_009", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_010", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_011", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_012", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_013", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_014", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_015", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_016", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_017", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_018", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_019", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_020", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_021", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_022", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_023", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_024", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_025", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_026", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_027", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_028", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_029", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_030", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_031", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_032", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_033", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_034", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_035", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_036", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_037", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_038", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_039", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_040", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_041", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_042", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_043", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_044", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_045", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_046", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_047", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_048", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_049", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_050", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_051", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_053", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_052", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_054", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_055", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_056", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_057", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_058", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_059", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_060", "status": "missed"}], "questions": [{"id": "question_01", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks for (a) the work plan the agent set at the start and (b) what the tooling probe in that same setup command revealed about available document libraries. Per cards 0003 and 0004, the agent created /app/workspace/process/ and encoded a sequenced plan in process/session-note-01.md (read the client email first, then each source, extract borrower-unfavorable issues, draft issue-memorandum.docx, verify), and in that same command probed tooling: pandoc found at /usr/bin/pandoc, python3 at /usr/local/bin/python3, and `python3 -c \"import docx\"` failing with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx', forcing pandoc/zipfile-XML extraction. The report gives none of this. It never mentions the work plan, the process directory, session-note-01.md, the probe, its paths, or the import failure. The only gesture is the Intake bullet: \"The agent read the assignment email and the client-priority notes, then converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc. The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML.\" That is a consequence-level remark about extraction methods (and it bundles in openpyxl, which the cited cards do not establish), not the probe's findings, and the plan half of the question is absent entirely. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct on the core content; I confirm the answer is missing from the report.", "evidence": ["card_0003", "card_0004"]}, {"id": "question_02", "answer": "With python-docx unavailable, the agent used pandoc as its toolchain: it read the Word sources by converting them to markdown with pandoc (parsing the Excel model directly from its XML since openpyxl was also unavailable), drafted the memo as markdown, then produced the final docx by converting the markdown back to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc, using a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt). Next it verified the result: it round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources (265 checks, 15 failures on the first run, which it judged to be formatting/checker errors and re-ran), repaired a missing Issue 5 body section, and then closed out by writing a verification session note, confirming the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc, and sending a final summary to the user.", "quote": "The agent read the assignment email and the client-priority notes, then converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc. The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "Card_0005 shows the agent reasoning that pandoc is available, that it can convert docx to markdown for reading, and that pandoc is simpler than pip-installing python-docx for both reading and writing, with the immediate plan to read all source documents first. The report communicates the same material answer: python-docx was unavailable, so pandoc was used to convert the Word sources to markdown for reading, and the final deliverable was produced by writing markdown and converting it to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc; it also says the agent read the assignment email first, matching the \"read all the source documents, starting with the assignment email\" next step. The report's additional detail (custom reference document, verification round-trip) is consistent with the rest of the trace and does not contradict the cited card.", "evidence": ["card_0005"]}, {"id": "question_03", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report never mentions the email's pandoc output being truncated (800 of 10205 chars visible) or the recovery step (python3 one-liner splitting the .eml and decoding the quoted-printable body with quopri into process/email-full.txt). Its only intake statement is the vague gesture \"The agent read the assignment email and the client-priority notes, then converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc,\" which presents the email as read in one pass and answers neither half of the question (what went wrong, how it was recovered). The report's two \"truncated\" mentions are explicitly about the loan agreement, not the email: \"Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. The agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading.\" A reader of the report would wrongly believe the email, including the CLIENT CONCERNS / PRIORITY FOCUS AREAS section that cards 0006–0008 show was initially lost to truncation, was read in a single pass. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0006", "card_0007", "card_0008"]}, {"id": "question_04", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report gestures at the transition but never gives the decision the question asks about. Its \"Intake\" item states only: \"The agent read the assignment email and the client-priority notes, then converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc.\" and its \"Reading the loan agreement\" item states: \"Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. The agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading.\" Section 5 repeats the truncation framing (\"The loan agreement was read in pieces\"). None of this conveys the trace-grounded reason found in card_0011 — that the draft loan agreement was a long 79KB docx, so the agent deliberately converted the sources to markdown and planned to read them in split line ranges rather than reading the raw docx — nor the mechanics in card_0012 (one pandoc --wrap=none command producing loan-agreement.md 1033 lines, guaranty.md 429, esa.md 227, appraisal.md 597 as the basis for chunked reads). The report's only causal claim attributes the line-range reading to truncated tool output, not to the document-length decision, so the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT call stands: the why is absent, and the how is only a bare topic mention.", "evidence": ["card_0009", "card_0010", "card_0011", "card_0012"]}, {"id": "question_05", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0013, card_0014) is that after converting all sources to markdown, the agent designated the loan agreement as the main document (1033 lines) and began the close reading chunk by chunk, opening with sed -n '1,180p' process/loan-agreement.md. The report never makes this designation: it never calls the loan agreement the main document, gives no line count, and shows no opening read or unit of close reading. Its only touches on the topic are the ordered-step note \"Reading the loan agreement (cards 0012–0045). Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. The agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading.\" and the caveat \"The loan agreement was read in pieces. Tool output was repeatedly truncated, so the agent re-read sections in ranges.\" Both describe a re-reading driven by truncation, not the answer to what was designated main and how the close reading began, so they are vague gestures that do not materially answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is therefore correct.", "evidence": ["card_0013", "card_0014"]}, {"id": "question_06", "answer": "The agent abandoned straight line-range paging because tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated, so it re-read the agreement in line ranges and by section heading (i.e., in pieces, since no single complete pass appears in the records).", "quote": "Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. The agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks why the agent abandoned straight line-range paging and what replacement strategy it adopted. The report answers both halves. Card_0016 shows a sed range read (180–400) whose output was truncated with the middle missing, and card_0017 records the agent's reasoning (\"The middle section is truncated... let me use grep to find the section headings and read the file in parts\"). The report states this as \"Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated\" (section 2) and repeats it in section 5 (\"Tool output was repeatedly truncated, so the agent re-read sections in ranges. The records do not show a single complete pass.\"). For the replacement strategy, card_0018 shows the agent grepping Section/Article headings to build a line-numbered heading map (ARTICLE I at line 35, Section 1.1 at line 37), and cards 0024/0026/0028 show the subsequent targeted reads (lines 228–292, 200–228, 399–445). The report renders this as \"re-read it in line ranges and by section heading,\" which captures the map-then-target pattern: reading by section heading plus targeted line ranges rather than blind paging. It does not spell out the line-numbered heading map or the specific ranges, but the material trace-grounded answer (truncated output was the cause; targeted, heading-driven re-reads were the replacement) is present, accurate, and uncontradicted, so the report communicates the answer.", "evidence": ["card_0016", "card_0017", "card_0018"]}, {"id": "question_07", "answer": "After reading the loan agreement (in line ranges and by section heading, since tool output was repeatedly truncated), the agent recorded first-pass issue notes, created a findings log (process/findings-log.md) with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard, then drafted an internal issue memorandum for partner Katherine Cheng (process/memo-draft.md, Sections I–XIII), refined it via scripted Python edits, converted it to Word (issue-memorandum.docx) with pandoc, and verified it with scripted checks (including a pandoc round-trip), fixing a defect where Issue 5 lacked a body section. It explicitly left several points pending: (1) the extension-test conclusion — the record does not show the first-extension DSCR point reconciled in the final text, and the memo's \"effectively three-year term\" conclusion depends on it; (2) a guaranty cross-reference (Issue 13 cited Guaranty §6.1(d) instead of §3.4) that the agent flagged and listed as a fix, but no later card shows the correction applied; plus other open items — statute citations not sourced in the record, no single complete pass over the loan agreement, the 18-month prepayment-lockout EOD question never checked, and unconfirmed origination-fee dollar amount.", "quote": "The agent recorded first-pass issue notes, then created process/findings-log.md with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question has two parts. (1) What the agent did with its analysis after the read-through: card_0043 shows the consolidated first-pass observation of the loan agreement (37 numbered key points, extension-trap analysis), and card_0044 shows the targeted re-verification of lines 343–395 (Article V escrow/reserves/cash-management) plus the document tail. The report's timeline states the agent \"recorded first-pass issue notes, then created process/findings-log.md with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard\" (citing card 0043) and that after truncated tool output \"the agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading\" — the consolidation and the range re-reads are both communicated, though the report does not name the specific Article V ranges. (2) Which conclusions were left pending: card_0043/card_0011 show the extension-conditions finding (stricter DSCR test at amortization kick-in vs. ~1.33x IO DSCR at the SOFR floor) held open pending reconciliation against the borrower's underwriting model. The report answers this directly: the lede says \"several points are unresolved, including the extension-test conclusion,\" and section 5 item 1 is titled \"The extension conclusion is open,\" explaining that the memo's three-year-term conclusion rests on the first-extension DSCR point, which \"the records I read do not show ... reconciled in the final text.\" The report-only reader's extraction was consistent with the report. Both material parts of the trace-grounded answer are present and carried by the report's own text.", "evidence": ["card_0043", "card_0044", "card_0011"]}, {"id": "question_08", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent worked out its \"extension trap\" conclusion and what numbers it used after the read-through. Per card_0043 (turn 46), the answer is: the agent traced the defined terms (Debt Service measured on the then-applicable amortization schedule, IO during the IO period; test run 30 days before maturity), so the 1.30x test is met on an IO basis — underwritten NOI $3,734,880 ÷ $2,808,400 (5.90% on $47.6M) = 1.33x — but once the extension's 30-year amortization kicks in, hand-computed P&I of ~$282,349/month (~$3,388,188/year) drops DSCR to ~1.10x, below the 1.25 ongoing covenant, i.e., extending triggers immediate covenant default; it flagged this as a \"serious trap\" in the same pass that settled the working issue catalog.\n\nThe report does not communicate any of this derivation or these numbers. The strings 2,808,400; 282,349; 3,388,188; 1.33x; 1.10x; \"serious trap\"; \"30 days before\" appear nowhere in sample_report.html (verified by search). The report only gestures at the topic: (1) \"Notes and findings log (cards 0043, 0068). The agent recorded first-pass issue notes, then created process/findings-log.md with 23 numbered items\" — citing the right card but explaining nothing about the trap; (2) \"The agent read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full, appended guaranty facts to the findings log, and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic.\" — a vague process mention with no numbers; and (3) a Section 5 item about a later, different (model-based, card_0077) analysis: \"The agent's own reasoning (card_0077) noted that the first extension DSCR test is probably measured on interest-only debt service. On that basis the Year 3 figure of about 1.345x would pass the 1.30x test. The Year 3 debt yield of 7.93% would still fail the 8.00% test. The memo's Issue 4 is titled as structurally unachievable and concludes that the effective term is three years.\" That passage is the report's only substantive treatment of the extension-test issue, and it describes the model-derived figures (1.345x from Year 3 model NOI, 1.17x/1.22x once amortization applies) and presents the conclusion as open rather than explaining the agent's defined-terms derivation and IO 1.33x / P&I 1.10x arithmetic from cards 0043/0044. It also omits the \"serious trap\" flagging and the issue-catalog screening. The reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct; the closest report content is a topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so the category is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0043", "card_0044"]}, {"id": "question_09", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks which conclusions the agent explicitly left pending after the loan-agreement read-through, and which missing input it named for each. Card_0043 shows four explicit open items: the extension-trap finding was conditional on the underwriting model (year-4 NOI ramp of ~$4.5M would lift P&I-basis DSCR back to ~1.33x); the replacement reserve ($300/unit/year, ~$93,600) and renovation budget ($11,111/unit x 225 units = $2.5M holdback) still needed comparison against the model; the ~$44.7M net-proceeds figure needed a cross-check against the model's sources and uses; and (card_0053) the guaranty review was incomplete, with Articles 6-9 still unread. The report gives none of this. It does gesture at the topic - the lede says \"Several points are unresolved, including the extension-test conclusion and a guaranty cross-reference the agent flagged but whose correction the record does not show,\" and Section 5 has items \"The extension conclusion is open.\" and \"A guaranty cross-reference was flagged but not confirmed as corrected.\" - but those are different open points: the report's extension item concerns whether the IO-basis first-extension test (Year 3 ~1.345x passing 1.30x, 7.93% debt yield failing 8.00%) was reconciled in the final text, not the model-contingency the agent flagged, and it never names the missing inputs (no year-4 NOI ramp, no underwriting-model cross-check, no $4.5M). Its guaranty item is about the §6.1(d) mis-citation in Issue 13, not the unread Articles 6-9. The report nowhere states the reserve/renovation figures or the $44.7M net proceeds were left pending verification (the $93,600 figure appears only as a finished memo issue in the severity table), and a search for \"Article 6\", \"holdback\", \"11,111\", \"net proceeds\", \"ramp\", \"cross-check\" finds nothing. Since the specific pending conclusions and their named missing inputs are absent, the reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct and the report scores 0.", "evidence": ["card_0043", "card_0053"]}, {"id": "question_10", "answer": "The discrepancy involved the Phase I ESA's project number: the memo's issue list includes, under \"general indemnity and drafting errors,\" a \"wrong ESA project number\" (i.e., the loan documents' reference to the Phase I ESA did not match the actual report), alongside the related environmental conflict where the model's \"No RECs identified\" entry conflicted with the ESA's single REC. The agent handled it by flagging it in the issue memorandum as a Medium-severity issue (each memo issue carrying a provision citation, explanation, severity, and recommended position); the drafting refinements also covered \"Phase I timing.\"", "quote": "general indemnity and drafting errors (wrong ESA project number, guaranty cross-references, a 60-day versus 90-day involuntary-bankruptcy cure mismatch, and the model's \"No RECs identified\" entry, which conflicts with the ESA's single REC).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The gold answer (per card_0056/card_0057) is that the agent caught the Phase I ESA project-number mismatch between the ESA (CES-2024-ATL-04187) and the loan agreement's \"Phase I ESA\" definition (CES-2024-08731), recorded it, and carried it into the issue memo as a drafting error (Medium). The report communicates this material answer: its severity table lists, under Medium issues, \"general indemnity and drafting errors (wrong ESA project number, ...)\", i.e., the loan documents' reference to the ESA carries the wrong project number, and it was flagged in the issue memorandum as a Medium-severity drafting issue. The report does not give the two specific project numbers or narrate the moment of detection (\"I'll keep a record\"), but it does state the concrete discrepancy found by comparing the ESA against the loan agreement and how the agent handled it (flagged in the memo), which matches what the cited cards show. The report-only reader's extraction matches the report and is supported by it, so no miss to note.", "evidence": ["card_0056", "card_0057"]}, {"id": "question_11", "answer": "With openpyxl (and python-docx) unavailable, the agent read the borrower underwriting model (the Excel file) by parsing the spreadsheet directly from its XML. From it it extracted the Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full (including Sheet 2's DSCR row in model order — 1.20x, 1.29x, 1.34x, 1.17x, 1.22x — with below-covenant status in Columns 1, 4 and 5), re-derived the DSCR arithmetic, relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI, and quoted returns figures from the Returns tab (Year 3: 1.04x and 1.3%; Year 5: 1.37x and 8.4%; 8.1% and 1.36x at the lender-reserve level).", "quote": "The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent actually read borrower-underwriting.xlsx with no openpyxl/xlrd installed, and what it extracted. The report states that openpyxl was unavailable and \"the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML,\" which is a faithful rendering of card_0063 (reasoning to write a stdlib xlsx parser because the file has no sharedStrings.xml and all strings are inline) and the zipfile + xml.etree.ElementTree script in card_0064; it omits the explicit stdlib module names and the no-sharedStrings/inline-strings detail, but the core claim that the read was a hand-rolled XML parse rather than a spreadsheet library is present and correct. On extraction, the report says the agent read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic (card_0064's dump of all four sheets; card_0066's dedicated re-run on Sheet 2), that it relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and Returns-tab NOI, and it reproduces extracted Sheet-2 data: the DSCR row 1.20x/1.29x/1.34x/1.17x/1.22x with below-covenant status in Columns 1, 4 and 5 (Figure 1 and the extension-test discussion), matching card_0066's DSCR, COVENANT COMPLIANCE and debt-service rows. It does not name the Renovation Budget tab, the inline-string handling, or the ~$3,378,720 debt-service step-up figure by number, but it does communicate the material trace-grounded answer to both halves of the question, and the report-only reader's extraction was accurate and well supported.", "evidence": ["card_0063", "card_0064", "card_0066"]}, {"id": "question_12", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT, but the report does answer the question. Its ordered work narrative (Section 2) shows the transition after the underwriting model work: the agent moved into issue analysis, creating process/findings-log.md as the draft issues log (Step 3, citing card 0068 — the same card that writes the log), cross-reading the deal terms against the borrower's model (Step 4: \"read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full, appended guaranty facts to the findings log, and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic\"), and identifying severity-ranked borrower-unfavorable issues (lede: \"identifying 23 borrower-unfavorable issues: 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium\"; Section 3 lists them with provision citations, including \"Extension conditions the model shows failing\"). This matches card_0065 (model just extracted, moving on), card_0067 (cross-reading the loan agreement against the underwriting model, severity-ranked issues, memo structure), and card_0068 (the findings-log write with CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM tiers and section citations). The report does not spell out \"this file marks the end of this phase\" or the memo-structure planning, but the two material elements — the move into issue analysis and the findings log as the phase's persisted artifact — are carried by the report's own text. Reader miss noted.", "evidence": ["card_0065", "card_0067", "card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_13", "answer": "The agent's scoping rule was to exclude provisions it judged market-standard: the findings log held 23 numbered issue items plus a separate list of provisions deemed market-standard, and those market-standard provisions were deliberately kept out of the issue list because the assignment/partner's instruction was that market-standard terms not be flagged (not to be redlined). The unflagged provisions were carried into the memo as Section XII, \"provisions the agent reviewed but did not flag.\"", "quote": "The agent recorded first-pass issue notes, then created process/findings-log.md with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what scoping rule the agent applied to the draft findings log — which provisions were deliberately kept out of the issue list, and why. The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer: the assignment's deliverable required flagging only borrower-side problems and said \"Market-standard terms were not to be flagged\"; the report then says the agent's findings log held the 23 numbered issues \"and a list of provisions it judged market-standard,\" and that \"Section XII of the memo lists provisions the agent reviewed but did not flag, consistent with the partner's instruction not to redline market terms.\" Together these give both the rule (only non-market / borrower-unfavorable terms go in the issue list; market-standard terms are deliberately excluded and recorded separately) and the reason (the client/partner instruction not to flag standard terms), matching card_0067/card_0068, where the findings log carries an explicit \"NOT flagged as issues (market/standard, per email caution)\" exclusion list. The report does not enumerate the individual excluded provisions (hard lockbox, 5% late charge, jury waiver, 0.75% origination fee, etc.); it points to where they are carried (the findings-log list and memo Section XII) rather than naming them. That enumeration is omitted, but the scoping rule and its rationale are stated explicitly and are carried by the report's own text, so a reader would not mistake the exclusions for oversight.", "evidence": ["card_0067", "card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_14", "answer": "The report does not describe an explicit weighing of multiple tools for the Word deliverable. It records what was used and the one constraint on tooling: the agent chose pandoc — drafting the memo as markdown, building a custom pandoc reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt), and converting the markdown to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc. The only other Word tooling mentioned, the Python python-docx library, was unavailable (alongside openpyxl), which is the stated reason it could not be used; the report gives no further stated rationale than that availability constraint and the custom-styled reference document.", "quote": "The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0067, turn 70) is the agent's explicit deliberation: \"For the document generation, I'm deciding between using pandoc with a reference docx or installing python-docx via pip. Since python-docx gives me precise control over heading levels, bold formatting, and table styling without the unpredictability of pandoc's formatting, I'll go with that approach and verify the output is readable.\" The report nowhere conveys this weighing or rationale. Its only tooling statements are that \"The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML\" (a constraint on reading the Excel model, not on producing the Word file) and that \"The agent built a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt) and converted the markdown to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc.\" That second sentence records what was done, not a considered choice, and the report gives no stated rationale beyond the custom reference document. It never mentions pip, the formatting-control trade-off (heading levels, bold text, table styling), the \"unpredictability of pandoc's formatting\" concern, or the plan to verify the output is readable. The report-only reader correctly concluded the weighing is absent (its answer describes the report's pandoc-based conversion and the availability constraint, and I located both of its quotes verbatim). Since the material answer — the options weighed, the choice, and the stated rationale — is absent, this is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0067"]}, {"id": "question_15", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent's numerical cross-check of the loan agreement against the borrower's underwriting model confirmed and where the results were recorded. Per card_0067 and card_0068, the agent recomputed the key figures (IO debt service $2,808,400, 30-year amortizing debt service $3,378,720, DSCR 1.33x in-place, 1.34x Year 3, 1.17x Year 4 failing the 1.30x test, 1.22x Year 5; debt yields 7.85%/7.93%/8.30%; LTV 71.26%; fees $238,000/$357,000/$119,000), matched them to the model, and recorded a \"Cross-checks done\" section in process/findings-log.md, confirming the extension is unachievable on the borrower's own numbers. The report contains none of this. The closest it comes is a one-line step description that \"The agent read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full, appended guaranty facts to the findings log, and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic,\" and a description of the findings log as containing \"23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard.\" That is a vague topic mention: it neither states what the cross-check confirmed (that the loan-agreement figures match the model, including the failing extension DSCR figures) nor locates the recorded results in a cross-check section of findings-log.md. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0067", "card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_16", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report conveys the material trace-grounded answer. The Medium (8) row of the memo-findings table names all three discrepancies from card_0067/card_0068's cross-checks: the wrong ESA project number in the loan agreement's \"Phase I ESA\" definition (CES-2024-08731 vs. CES-2024-ATL-04187), the model's \"No RECs identified\" entry conflicting with the ESA's single REC (former dry cleaner at 4870 Briarcliff Crossing Drive), and the 60-day versus 90-day involuntary-bankruptcy mismatch between the loan and the Guaranty. The report frames these as issues recorded in the memo/findings log, matching the gold disposition (items 20 and 21 in process/findings-log.md). It is less granular than the gold: it does not cite the specific findings-log item numbers (20, 21), the exact project numbers, or the §4.5/§10.1(h) rep risk, and it attributes the discrepancies to the memo's content rather than narrating the cross-check step itself. That is a matter of detail, not of substance: a reader learns that all three inter-document discrepancies surfaced and were dispositioned into the issue list. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT answer is a miss; the content is plainly present in the Medium (8) row.", "evidence": ["card_0067", "card_0068"]}, {"id": "question_17", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer is a specific pre-drafting verification event: after building the findings log, the agent ran a targeted grep pass over the markdown conversions of the loan agreement, ESA, and guaranty (card_0070) to re-confirm the exact citations it planned to use - the Phase I ESA project-number mismatch (CES-2024-08731 in the LA versus CES-2024-ATL-04187 in the ESA), the Guaranty's wrong cross-references (\"Transfer\" incorporated from \"Section 8.2 of the Loan Agreement\" although Transfer is defined in LA §1.1, and the NOI determination referencing financial statements \"pursuant to Section 5.4\" although that is the Replacement Reserve and reporting is §6.1), the 60-day (LA §10.1(i)(ii)) versus 90-day (Guaranty §2.2(e)) involuntary-bankruptcy dismissal mismatch, and the model's \"No RECs identified\" line versus the ESA's one REC. Every check confirmed the discrepancy, so the agent recorded \"All verified\" (card_0071) and carried the confirmed citations into the memo.\n\nThe report never describes that pre-drafting re-verification. Its only verification coverage is the later, post-drafting work: \"The agent round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources. The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures.\" Its statement that \"The later checks confirmed that cited sections exist, which would not by itself catch a wrong subsection\" refers to those later existence checks, not to the pre-drafting grep pass or what it confirmed. Its only pre-drafting step, \"Model and guaranty checks,\" describes reading the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs and re-deriving the DSCR arithmetic - not re-checking planned citations against the source documents. The substantive discrepancies do appear, but only as memo findings in the Medium-severity row (\"general indemnity and drafting errors (wrong ESA project number, guaranty cross-references, a 60-day versus 90-day involuntary-bankruptcy cure mismatch, and the model's \"No RECs identified\" entry, which conflicts with the ESA's single REC)\"), which communicates what the memo says rather than which checks the agent re-ran against the underlying documents before drafting and what those checks confirmed. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is therefore correct.", "evidence": ["card_0069", "card_0070", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_18", "answer": "Per the report's ordered phase list, the agent moved into drafting after compiling its findings log (23 numbered items plus the market-standard list) and completing the model and guaranty checks: it then wrote the memo as a markdown file (process/memo-draft.md, about 280 lines, Sections I–XIII) and refined it through scripted Python edits. It generated the .docx from that markdown with pandoc (using a custom reference document) because the Python libraries for building the file directly were unavailable: \"The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable.\"", "quote": "Notes and findings log ( cards 0043, 0068 ). The agent recorded first-pass issue notes, then created process/findings-log.md with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer on both prongs of the question. On the transition: its ordered phase list places \"Drafting\" right after the findings log and the model/guaranty checks (the evidence-gathering phase), with the agent writing the full markdown memo (process/memo-draft.md) in that step, and Section 3 confirms the memo was organized by topic with a severity and a recommended position for each issue — matching card_0071's \"organize by topic area with severity and recommended negotiation positions\" and card_0072's immediate write of the full memo. On the docx mechanism: the report says the memo was converted from markdown to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc, and states that the python-docx (and openpyxl) libraries were unavailable, i.e. the constraint-driven reason from card_0071 (\"no python-docx available, so I'll build via pandoc from markdown\") rather than a stylistic preference. Minor gap: the report does not quote the \"All verified\" grep pass or the agent's explicit judgment that it had comprehensive evidence before drafting; it simply sequences drafting after the completed findings/checks. That omission is immaterial to the answer, which is carried by the report's own text. The report-only reader's extraction is accurate and its \"complete\" status is justified.", "evidence": ["card_0071", "card_0072"]}, {"id": "question_19", "answer": "The issue memorandum (issue-memorandum.docx, ~8,230 words, Sections I–XIII) was an internal memo to partner Katherine Cheng identifying 23 borrower-unfavorable issues — 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium — grouped by topic, each with a provision citation, an explanation, a severity and a recommended position; its headline conclusion was that the draft is effectively full recourse (Section XII lists unflagged market-standard provisions, Section XIII sets three-tier negotiation priorities). Its write was confirmed by converting the markdown to Word with pandoc, then round-tripping the Word file to plain text and running scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases (a first suite of 265 checks with 15 failures judged formatting/checker errors, re-run, plus a second suite of 164 checks). After fixing a defect (a missing body section for Issue 5), it re-checked the document, and at close-out it confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words) and wrote a verification session note.", "quote": "It produced an internal issue memorandum for partner Katherine Cheng ( issue-memorandum.docx ) identifying 23 borrower-unfavorable issues: 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium. The record shows the memorandum was drafted, converted to Word format and checked with scripted tests and a pandoc round-trip.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "Card_0072 shows the write actually happened this way: a single in-command heredoc creating the complete memo at process/memo-draft.md, self-verified with `echo \"wrote $(wc -l < process/memo-draft.md) lines\"`, which printed \"wrote 281 lines\" (exit 0). The memo as written held 22 numbered severity-rated issues, a summary table, the headline conclusion that the draft as written is not a non-recourse loan, the deliberately-not-flagged customary provisions section, and three-tier negotiation priorities. The report communicates that scope well (severity-rated issues with provision citations, full-recourse headline, Section XII not-flagged list, Section XIII tiers), but it misdescribes the write and its confirmation. It reports \"about 280 lines\" only as a description of the file, never mentions the in-command line-count echo or the \"wrote 281 lines\" output, and answers \"how was the write confirmed\" with later verification of the final Word file (pandoc round-trip and scripted citation/figure checks) instead of the write-time self-verification. It also says the memo identified 23 issues (the post-edit final document including added Issue 13A), not the 22 that card_0072's write produced. The report therefore addresses the question but is not carried by the trace for the confirmation prong, and the report-only reader's extracted answer inherits the same confirmation misdescription.", "evidence": ["card_0072"]}, {"id": "question_20", "answer": "The report says the agent did not resolve the year-by-year alignment. Because the model's column labels do not map cleanly to years, the DSCR values were treated positionally only, and the agent instead relied on the model's own covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI. For the extension-period analysis, the memo's Issue 4 is titled as structurally unachievable and concludes the effective term is three years, framing the 1.30x DSCR test as failing in the extension periods (about 1.17x and 1.22x once amortization applies). The report notes, however, that the agent's own reasoning said the first extension DSCR test is probably measured on interest-only debt service (Year 3 ~1.345x would pass 1.30x, but 7.93% debt yield still fails 8.00%), and that this first-extension point was not reconciled in the final text — so the report treats the extension conclusion as open.", "quote": "The DSCR row has five values against six labelled columns, and the Returns tab labels exits as Years 3–5. The agent relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent resolved which year each of the five DSCR values belongs to and what framing it adopted for the extension-period analysis. The report conveys the material answer: the agent re-derived the DSCR arithmetic from the model's own rows (the step card_0073 shows in detail, matching NOI/DS quotients to each DSCR value), and, because the DSCR row has five values against six labelled columns and the labels are inconsistent across tabs, it relied on the model's own covenant-compliance block and Returns-tab exit NOI rather than the ambiguous column headers — exactly the cross-check and labeling rationale in card_0076's findings-log entry. For the framing, the report states the extension analysis is presented in terms of \"the extension periods\" (1.17x/1.22x once amortization applies) rather than specific year labels, consistent with card_0076's \"the two extension periods\" phrasing. The report omits the explicit quotients (e.g., $3,948,604 / $3,378,720 = 1.17x) and the memo's verbatim \"two extension periods\" quote, but the method (arithmetic re-derivation plus reliance on the model's own compliance flags/Returns-tab NOI) and the extension-periods framing are both communicated, so a report-only reader can tell the figures were arithmetically verified rather than guessed positionally and why. The report's separate caveat that the first-extension point is unreconciled is an additional observation about memo quality, not a contradiction of this answer.", "evidence": ["card_0073", "card_0076"]}, {"id": "question_21", "answer": "The agent produced issue-memorandum.docx because that was the deliverable assigned to it: an internal memorandum to partner Katherine Cheng (due end of day Monday, February 24, 2025) identifying borrower-unfavorable issues. It decided to build it by first writing a markdown draft (process/memo-draft.md, about 280 lines, Sections I–XIII), refining it through scripted Python edits, then converting the markdown to Word with pandoc using a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt). For organization and calibration, the plan was that each issue must cite the provision, explain the borrower-side problem, be assigned a severity (Critical, High or Medium), and carry a concrete counter-position, with market-standard terms explicitly not flagged. The memo groups its 23 issues by topic (headlined by the conclusion that the draft is effectively full recourse), uses a severity summary table, reserves Section XII for provisions reviewed but not flagged (consistent with the no-market-terms instruction), and Section XIII for negotiation priorities in three tiers. It calibrated the final content with verification: a pandoc round-trip to plain text plus scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases (first run: 265 checks, 15 failures judged as formatting/checker errors, then a second suite of 164 checks), and it repaired a defect where the summary table listed Issue 5 but the rendered memo lacked its body section.", "quote": "Deliverable. An internal memorandum to Katherine Cheng, due end of day Monday, February 24, 2025. For each issue the memo had to cite the provision, explain the borrower-side problem, assign a severity (Critical, High or Medium) and propose a concrete counter-position. Market-standard terms were not to be flagged.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent decided to produce the docx and how it planned to organize and calibrate the memo. The report carries the material answer grounded in the cited cards: it shows python-docx (and openpyxl) were unavailable (card_0075's decision to fall back to markdown+pandoc because python-docx was not installed) and that the memo was written as markdown and converted with pandoc using a custom reference document, with the close-out confirming a well-formed Office file. On organization, it states the memo is an internal memorandum to Katherine Cheng (the lede establishes the agent worked as Robert Navarro) due February 24, 2025, grouped by topic, with each issue given a provision citation, explanation, severity and recommended position — matching the four-element structure in card_0075. On calibration, it gives the severity mix (4 Critical, 11 High, 8 Medium), explicitly reconciles the planned \"High 10\" against the 11 issues actually listed (the extra High is Issue 13A), and ties the not-flagged list (findings log and Section XII) to the partner's instruction not to flag market-standard terms, as in card_0073 and card_0076. What it omits is detail, not the substance: the client's ten named topic areas and the two specific content refinements (folding the SPE adequate-capital point into Issue 6, and the net-worth mismatch wording LA §7.3 vs Guaranty §3.1) are not spelled out. Its \"23 issues / 11 High\" figures differ from the gold's planning-time \"22 issues / 4-10-8\" only by the later-added Issue 13A, which the report itself explains, so nothing contradicted or unsupported.", "evidence": ["card_0073", "card_0075", "card_0076"]}, {"id": "question_22", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Cards 0073, 0075 and 0076 show that before drafting/converting the memo, the agent appended three consolidated blocks to process/findings-log.md as build inputs: (1) the confirmed additional guaranty facts (G §2.2 liability, §2.3 waivers, §7.1/§7.2 remedies and costs, §8.1 unilateral modification, §9.5 assignment, §9.11 reinstatement, §5.2/§9.12 environmental indemnity, §3.3 EOD, plus the LA §7.3 vs G §3.1/§3.2 and 60-day vs 90-day bankruptcy mismatches); (2) the DSCR re-verification for the two extension periods (1.17x and 1.22x against the 1.30x test, relying on the model's COVENANT COMPLIANCE flags and returns-tab exit NOI because the model's labels are inconsistent); and (3) the deliberately not-flagged market-standard list (hard lockbox, 2-month escrow cushion, reporting cadence, origination fee, 18-month prepay lockout, 5% late charge, extension fee, $500k judgment EOD, 30-day cure, GA law/venue/jury waiver, tax-parcel covenant, books/records, title/UCC CPs). The report never gives this consolidation. It only gestures at the topic in one generic step line — \"appended guaranty facts to the findings log, and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic\" — without naming what the consolidated blocks were, tying them to the docx build, giving the extension-period DSCR results, or mentioning the not-flagged list in this context (the market-standard list appears only earlier, as part of the initial 23-item log creation). The DSCR values 1.17x/1.22x do appear, but only as the memo's Issue 4 conclusion in the findings table, not as a log consolidation. This is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0073", "card_0075", "card_0076"]}, {"id": "question_23", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the consolidated grep pass against the converted loan agreement (process/loan-agreement.md, card_0078) confirmed and what specific memo-refinement action followed each confirmed fact (card_0079: cite the \"successor administrator\" wording to confirm Issue 18's absence finding; add the Guaranty §2.2 \"yield maintenance\" cross-document mismatch as a drafting bullet; note LA §4.5(c) already acknowledges the REC so the model's \"no RECs\" entry is the error, and fix the ESA project-number citation). The report gives none of this. It never mentions the grep pass or cards 0078/0079, and searching the report for \"grep\", \"yield maintenance\", \"successor\", or \"4.5\" returns no hits. The only related material is a topic-level mention in the memo-summary table — \"missing ARRC-style SOFR fallback\" and, among drafting errors, \"wrong ESA project number\" and \"the model's \\\"No RECs identified\\\" entry, which conflicts with the ESA's single REC\" — which states the memo's findings but does not say these absence/mismatch claims were verified directly against the loan-agreement text, omits the yield-maintenance finding entirely, and identifies no per-fact action (e.g., citing the \"successor administrator\" wording, the §4.5(c) consistency point, or the §4.5(b) project-number fix). Those vague topic mentions do not materially answer what the verification confirmed or what the agent did about each fact, so the answer is missing from the report.", "evidence": ["card_0078", "card_0079"]}, {"id": "question_24", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent learned from Schedule 1 (the loan agreement's objective, step-by-step NOI methodology fixing Underwritten NOI at $3,734,880 and expressly excluding capex, debt service, depreciation, amortization and taxes — card_0080) and what it therefore added to Issue 1 (that Guaranty §2.2(b) lets the Lender recalculate NOI \"in its sole and absolute discretion,\" including adjustments for exactly those excluded line items, with a no-contest waiver — a direct conflict, and that the memo should insist the Guaranty conform to Schedule 1, deleting the sole-discretion override and waiver — card_0081). The report never conveys the substance of this. It mentions the topic only in passing: once, as a list item in the drafting step (\"The edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict...\"), and once, in the summary table (\"The memo also flags a conflict with the Schedule 1 NOI methodology.\"). A grep for the material content — recalculate/discretion/conform/waiver/capex/line items and the Schedule 1 figures — finds nothing; \"sole discretion\" appears only regarding property-manager consent (Issue 12), a different issue. This is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer either half of the question (what was learned, what was added, or the recommended fix), so the answer is missing_from_report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0080", "card_0081"]}, {"id": "question_25", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks (a) why the agent rewrote memo Issue 4's \"extension trap\" analysis instead of keeping its earlier framing, and (b) what two structural facts the rewritten analysis rests on. Per card_0081 and card_0082, the agent re-examined the §2.6(b) measurement date (\"30 days prior to the then-current Maturity Date\"), realized the DSCR test is measured on different debt-service bases for the two extensions (First Extension measured on interest-only $2,808,400 debt service, passing at ~1.34x; Second Extension measured after §2.3(b) 30-year amortization has stepped debt service up to ~$3,378,720, failing at ~1.17x/1.22x), and rewrote Issue 4 around two structural facts: (1) the LTV ≤ 70% condition on a new lender-ordered MAI appraisal starts already failing (71.26% at closing; ~$68M of value needed), and (2) debt service steps up at exactly the moment the DSCR test bites. The report never states that Issue 4 was rewritten or why. It only lists \"extension LTV and amortization wording\" among the scripted edits, and its item 5.1 (\"The agent's own reasoning (card_0077) noted that the first extension DSCR test is probably measured on interest-only debt service. On that basis the Year 3 figure of about 1.345x would pass the 1.30x test.\") presents the trigger insight as an open point the record does not show as reconciled in the final text (\"The records I read do not show that the first-extension point was reconciled in the final text, and that conclusion depends on it.\") — the opposite of the actual rewrite. The findings table does convey the substance of the two facts (\"the 70% LTV test fails at opening (47.6M ÷ 66.8M = 71.26%), and the 1.30x DSCR test fails in the extension periods (about 1.17x and 1.22x once amortization applies)\"), but as the memo's stated conclusion preserving the earlier both-fail framing, not as the basis of a rewritten analysis. This is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is essentially correct; the miss is noted here.", "evidence": ["card_0081", "card_0082"]}, {"id": "question_26", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (per card_0082) is that the agent applied the refinements with a single python3 heredoc script performing anchored string replacements, each guarded by an assert (e.g., \"Issue1 anchor not found\"), chosen to avoid shell-escaping pain on a long markdown file with quotes and dollar figures, which succeeded in one pass and printed \"edits applied; lines: 287\". The report addresses this only with a vague topic mention: in its drafting step it says \"The agent wrote process/memo-draft.md (about 280 lines, Sections I–XIII), then refined it through scripted Python edits. The edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict, extension LTV and amortization wording, the scope paragraph and dates, the default-rate and exit-fee notes, Phase I timing, and a new Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause.\" That names a generic \"scripted Python edits\" mechanism and lists the subjects of the edits, but it does not say how the edits were applied (no heredoc, no anchored/assert-guarded replacements, no one-pass success or 281-to-287 line growth) or why that mechanism was chosen (no \"avoid shell-escaping pain\" or any rationale at all). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct; a bare mechanism mention that omits both the how and the why is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so it is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0081", "card_0082"]}, {"id": "question_27", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report does not answer either part of the question. It never names the two conflicting Year-5 NOI figures ($4,128,030 vs $4,948,604), never states which value was kept in the memo, and never gives the decision rationale (card_0083 shows the agent kept the returns tab's $4,128,030 because the returns tab's 8.67% debt yield equals $4,128,030 divided by the $47.6M loan; card_0084 shows that figure carried into the edits). The report only gestures at the neighborhood: item 5 of section 5 notes the agent \"relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI,\" which points at the returns tab in general, and the drafting step lists \"The edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict\" among scripted edits — but that refers to a Schedule 1 methodology conflict, and in neither place are the specific figures or the debt-yield consistency check that carried the decision. A vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_28", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT answer is correct. The gold answer, per card_0083 and card_0084, is that the agent realized its \"15-day/180-day update\" wording conflated two distinct requirements — the CERCLA \"all appropriate inquiries\" 180-day refresh window and the customary lender 15-day Phase I update — and that it rewrote Issue 20 to say the March 14, 2025 closing falls within the 180-day window (to roughly May 21, 2025) so the report is not yet stale for CERCLA purposes, while recommending the standard 15-day update as a closing deliverable. The report does not communicate any of this. The only related mention is a vague topic reference in the drafting summary: \"The edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict, extension LTV and amortization wording, the scope paragraph and dates, the default-rate and exit-fee notes, Phase I timing, and a new Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause.\" That single phrase \"Phase I timing\" does not state what error was found in the 15-day/180-day language, does not identify the CERCLA/AAI window versus customary lender practice, and does not describe the rewrite; it is a bare topic mention that does not materially answer the question. No 180-day window, AAI, staleness analysis, or 15-day-update-as-closing-deliverable recommendation appears anywhere in the report.", "evidence": ["card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_29", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question has three prongs: (1) the provision that prompted Issue 13A, (2) the recommended position taken, and (3) where the issue appeared in the memo. The report only gestures at prongs 1 and 3 and gives nothing for prong 2. It says the refinement pass added \"a new Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause,\" lists \"Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause (Guaranty §8.1)\" among the High issues, and notes \"It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.\" Those citations are verbatim in the report (section 2, items 5 and 8, and the Section 3 High row), but they do not state the triggering text of §8.1 (Lender may modify any Loan Document in its sole and absolute discretion without notice or consent), the read-with §§2.3(c) and 9.11, or — decisively — the recommended position, which card_0083/card_0084 show as notice to the guarantor of modifications plus the standard \"no increase\" protection for post-closing increases in principal or interest rate. No passage in the report describes that position; a search of the report finds no such language. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is corroborated. Because the report does not materially answer the question (the substance of the new issue's position is absent), it is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_30", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent settled the DSCR row's year attributions and what that meant for the memo's Issue 9. Per cards 0085-0087, the agent parsed the raw sheet2.xml of borrower-underwriting.xlsx with cell references (D50=1.20x under \"Year 1 (2025-2026)\", G50=1.17x under \"Year 4 / Ext 1\", H50=1.22x under \"Year 5 / Ext 2\", with the covenant rows aligned BELOW/BELOW/BELOW) and concluded the Issue 9 phrasing (\"DSCR as low as 1.20x in the first full year\", BELOW flags in the extension periods) was consistent with the model's own labels and required no change. The report never communicates that resolution: it contains no cell-reference check, no year attribution for the three values, and no statement about Issue 9 being verified as consistent. Instead, the report takes a contrary position: its Figure 1 caption asserts \"The model's labels do not map cleanly to years, so the bars are shown by position only,\" and section 5 lists the misalignment (\"The DSCR row has five values against six labelled columns\") as an open internal inconsistency the agent simply \"relied on\" around, with no settling of which year each value belongs to or any consequence for Issue 9. The only XML mention in the report is a general intake note that the model \"was parsed directly from its XML\" because openpyxl was unavailable, which is not the card_0085-0087 verification. The report thus addresses the DSCR-row topic but gives an account contradicted by the trace (labels unresolved vs. resolved by raw cell references; no Issue 9 conclusion vs. \"consistent, no change needed\"). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT status is roughly right in that the material answer is absent, but because the report affirmatively misstates the resolution, unsupported_or_contradicted fits better than a plain missing answer.", "evidence": ["card_0085", "card_0086", "card_0087"]}, {"id": "question_31", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what exactly tripped the sandbox guard on the first reference-document build (the \"rm -rf refx\" in the rejected command, flagged with the message \"rm -f style commands are not permitted. Use a safer approach\") and how the agent rebuilt it (fresh /tmp/refx2 directory with \"mkdir -p\", no rm, unzip -oq, same styles.xml patch, rezip to /tmp/ref-custom.docx, confirmed by \"styles patched\" and ls at 11,739 bytes — card_0088, card_0089, card_0090). The report never addresses the rejection, the guard message, or the rebuild. Its only treatment of this span is a single line that mentions the end result of the topic, quoted in the rationale as: \"The agent built a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt) and converted the markdown to <span class=\"ref\">issue-memorandum.docx<\/span> with pandoc.\" — a vague mention that omits the failure, its cause, and the workaround entirely. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT matches the report's actual contents.", "evidence": ["card_0088", "card_0089", "card_0090"]}, {"id": "question_32", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question has two parts. The report handles the first: it says the agent converted the memo markdown to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc using a custom Times New Roman reference document (\"The agent built a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt) and converted the markdown to <span class=\\\"ref\\\">issue-memorandum.docx<\/span> with pandoc.\"), consistent with cards 0087, 0091, and 0092. But the second part — what happened to the file-type verification step in the same command — is absent. Per card_0092, the chained \"file issue-memorandum.docx\" check failed with \"/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found\" (exit 127), so the docx file-type check never actually ran at build time. The report never mentions this failure: \"command not found\", \"exit 127\", and any reference to the in-command \"file\" check do not appear anywhere in the report. The only file-type-adjacent statement is the later close-out line (\"confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words)\"), which describes a separate later verification and does not say what happened to the in-command check; if anything it could mislead a reader into thinking the file type was confirmed at build time, the exact misreading the question targets. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is corroborated for the material part of the answer, so the report scores on its own (absent) coverage.", "evidence": ["card_0087", "card_0091", "card_0092"]}, {"id": "question_33", "answer": "After creating issue-memorandum.docx, the agent declared a Verification phase, in which it round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources. The first run had 265 checks with 15 failures, which the agent judged to be formatting differences or checker errors; it re-ran the suite and later ran a second suite of 164 checks. At close-out it confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words).", "quote": "Verification ( cards 0093–0107, 0122, 0126–0134 ). The agent round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources. The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report addresses both halves of the question. It names the declared Verification phase and describes its design: a pandoc round-trip of issue-memorandum.docx to plain text followed by scripted cross-checks of section citations, figures, and quoted phrases against the extracted source documents — matching the plan in card_0093 and card_0095 (round-trip, then an automated check of every citation, figure, name and date). For what the first checks showed, the report states the round-trip produced plain text and that the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (Close-out item), i.e., the round-trip check passed and the deliverable was found intact before fact-level checking, consistent with card_0094 (pandoc exit 0, readable output, expected memo header). The report omits the fine-grained round-trip details (293 lines, grep -c \"Issue\" = 30, header contents) and does not mention the full read-through, but it conveys the material answer: the declared verification design and that the first integrity check succeeded.", "evidence": ["card_0093", "card_0094", "card_0095"]}, {"id": "question_34", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent decided to do with the 15 failures from the 265-item fact-check and the four memo edits it made. The report only gestures at the topic. In Section 2 it says \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks,\" and in Section 5 it repeats \"The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265. The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error.\" That generic attribution does not convey the trace's decision: per card_0097 and card_0099, the agent triaged all 15 failures item by item and concluded 11 were checker false positives (markdown escaping of apostrophes/dollar signs, format differences like \"1.25 to 1.00\" vs \"1.25x\" and \"ninety (90) days\") while 4 were real memo gaps, and then fixed the memo rather than relaxing the checks. The four concrete edits (exact $42,000,000/$9,800,000 guarantor net-worth/liquidity figures plus headroom math; interest-rate detail in the intro — daily simple SOFR + 2.65%, 3.25% SOFR floor, 5.90% minimum; CGL expanded to $2,000,000 per occurrence / $5,000,000 aggregate; and the 4.0% management-fee cap added to Issue 12) appear nowhere in the report — I verified the report contains none of these figures or terms. The reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0096", "card_0097", "card_0098", "card_0099"]}, {"id": "question_35", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 0100-0102) is: the four triage edits were applied to process/memo-draft.md via an assert-guarded Python replacement script, followed by pandoc regeneration of issue-memorandum.docx (exit 0, \"ok\"/\"regenerated\"), and the re-run of the 15-assertion verification (pandoc round-trip to memo-rt.txt plus a Python check of 8 memo figures and 7 source-format strings) came back \"SOME FAIL\" — 12 of 15 passed, with \"$119,000\", \"$357,000\", and the loan-agreement interest-rate-cap check (\"strike rate of 3.50%\" + quoted \"A\") still failing, against an expectation that all 15 would be resolved. The report never states the re-run result or the expected-vs-actual comparison. It only gestures at the topic: \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.\" (Verification bullet) and \"The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265. The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error.\" (section 5). No \"SOME FAIL\", no 12-of-15, no 119k/357k/IR-cap failure list appears anywhere; the only 357,000 mention is an unrelated caveat that \"The record does not confirm that the origination-fee dollar amount ($357,000) was added to the memo\". The report also never identifies the four triage-specific edits or the assert-guarded script + pandoc regeneration mechanism (its \"Drafting\" bullet lists a different, earlier set of edits). A reader of the report could not learn what the re-verification actually returned, so the material answer is absent; the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0100", "card_0101", "card_0102"]}, {"id": "question_36", "answer": "The agent treated all remaining failures as artifacts rather than real defects: after the first scripted verification run (265 checks) produced 15 failures, it judged them to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and later ran a second, separate suite of 164 checks (along with the pandoc round-trip) before closing out; the report notes that \"verified\" in the memo means checked against the extracted text, not an independent confirmation.", "quote": "The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks for the agent's accounting of each remaining FAIL line and the specific re-checks it ran before declaring the facts verified. Per the cited cards, the agent diagnosed both remaining failures as checker artifacts: (1) the \"119k/357k in the memo\" lines were not defects because the memo cites the fees as percentages (0.25% extension, 0.75% origination), confirmed by a targeted grep of the rendered memo (card_0103, card_0104); and (2) the \"LA IR cap 3.50 + A\" line was an escaping artifact of the check script, with a re-check confirming the loan agreement contains \"strike rate of 3.50%\" plus the A-rated requirement (card_0104, card_0107), along with re-verification of the DSCR figures and the environmental-indemnity/guaranty quotes (card_0105, card_0107).\n\nThe report only gestures at this topic. It says in general terms: \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.\" and, in its caveats section, \"The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265. The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error. The record does not confirm that the origination-fee dollar amount ($357,000) was added to the memo, although the agent said it would consider this (<span class=\\\"ref\\\">card_0107<\/span>); the memo states the fee as a percentage.\"\n\nThat is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question: the report never identifies the remaining FAIL lines, gives no account of the second failure (the A-rated interest-rate-cap / escaped-quote artifact is absent entirely — no \"3.50\", cap, or escaping appears in the report), and the one failure it does touch (fee as percentage) is framed as an unresolved record caveat rather than as the agent's explanation of a FAIL line. Nor does it describe the specific re-checks (grep of the percentages in the rendered memo, re-check of \"strike rate of 3.50%\" with the A rating, re-verification of the DSCR and indemnity/guaranty quotes) — only a generic re-run and an unnamed \"second suite of 164 checks.\" The report-only reader's extracted answer largely restates that generic material and likewise misses the per-failure accounting and the concrete re-checks, so I score on the report: missing.", "evidence": ["card_0103", "card_0104", "card_0105", "card_0107"]}, {"id": "question_37", "answer": "The final read-through of the rendered memo revealed that the summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it. The agent established its scope by comparing the summary table to the rendered document: it inserted the missing section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document, also moving Issue 13A into its correct position.", "quote": "The summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it. The agent inserted the section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document. It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what defect the final read-through of the rendered memo revealed and how the agent established its scope and cause. The report conveys the defect itself: the summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger) while the rendered memo had no body section for it, matching card_0111 through card_0115 (grep of \"^Issue \" in process/memo-rt.txt showing only 22 headings with no Issue 5, and the follow-up grep showing \"Major-casualty full-recourse trigger (High)\" only as row 5 of the summary table on line 36). It also implies the scope through \"restored the 23 headings.\" However, the cause half of the question is absent: the trace shows the agent traced the gap to the markdown draft (card_0115, card_0116: no \"### Issue 5\" heading, list jumps 4 -> 6) and concluded it was its own editing mistake — card_0119: \"This was an editing mistake (I dropped it when I rewrote the draft).\" The report never states that the agent attributed the defect to its own earlier draft rewrite, nor does it describe the scope-establishment method (grep of the rendered text / markdown draft) beyond the outcome \"restored the 23 headings.\" A reader of the report would learn that Issue 5 was missing from the body and was added, but would not learn that the missing section originated in the agent's own earlier draft rewrite — the user_consequence explicitly flagged as the point of this question. Because the cause is not communicated, the report does not materially answer the question.", "evidence": ["card_0111", "card_0112", "card_0113", "card_0114", "card_0115"]}, {"id": "question_38", "answer": "The agent restored the missing Issue 5 section (the major-casualty recourse trigger, which was in the summary table but absent from the rendered memo's body) by inserting the section, which it placed before Section IV, and it also restored the 23 headings (and moved Issue 13A into its correct position). The evidence that the repair worked was that the agent re-checked the document after inserting the section.", "quote": "Defect found and repaired ( cards 0111–0129 ). The summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it. The agent inserted the section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document. It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report's \"Defect found and repaired\" item communicates the material trace-grounded answer to all three prongs of the question. How it was restored: the agent detected that Issue 5 was in the summary table but absent from the memo body and inserted the section into the draft (card_0117 shows the detection and placement deliberation; card_0120 shows the insertion of the reconstructed Issue 5 text before the \"## IV. TRANSFERS / CHANGE OF CONTROL\" anchor). Where it was placed: \"before Section IV\" matches the card evidence of insertion immediately before the Section IV TRANSFERS heading. What evidence confirmed the repair: \"restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document\" corresponds to card_0120's verification output, where the \"^Issue \" heading count read 23 after regeneration. The report omits finer details from the gold answer (that the content was rebuilt from the agent's earlier investigation, the assert-guarded Python replace, the \"Issue 5 inserted\" confirmation string, the 22-to-23 delta, and Issue 5 landing at line 119), but the compressed account it does give is correct, trace-grounded, and materially answers the question rather than merely gesturing at the topic.", "evidence": ["card_0117", "card_0119", "card_0120"]}, {"id": "question_39", "answer": "In the same pass that found the missing Issue 5, the agent also flagged that Issue 13A (the guaranty unilateral-modification clause issue) was not in its correct position in the memo. It stated it would fix this by moving Issue 13A into its correct position, and the report states the fix was actually carried out: the pass is titled \"Defect found and repaired\" and says the agent \"It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position,\" re-checking the document afterward.", "quote": "Defect found and repaired ( cards 0111–0129 ). The summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it. The agent inserted the section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document. It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "In the pass that found the missing Issue 5, the agent flagged a second inconsistency: Issue 13A appears after Issue 14 in the memo body while the summary table lists 13, 13A, 14, and it stated it would fix the ordering by moving 13A to between 13 and 14 in the Markdown draft (card_0111). That reordering was never made: the chunk's only file write was the Issue 5 insertion and docx regeneration (card_0120, following the grep in card_0116), and the later final verification still listed the document order with 13A after 14. The report, however, claims \"It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position,\" asserting a completed fix the trace does not show. It never describes the flagged inconsistency (body order 13 -> 14 -> 13A vs. table order 13, 13A, 14), the stated intended move, or that the fix was left pending; instead it contradicts the very cards it cites (0111–0129). The report-only reader's extraction mirrors the report's text but inherits this error. Scored on the report's own (incorrect) statement, this is unsupported_or_contradicted.", "evidence": ["card_0111", "card_0116", "card_0120"]}, {"id": "question_40", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent marked the repair phase complete and what the final full verification re-run reported. The material trace-grounded answer (cards 0121-0123) is: the agent declared \"All 23 issues are now present,\" noted Issue 5 now sits right after Issue 4 within section III (RECOUSE / GUARANTY), and moved to final closeout (re-run the full verification suite, check docx heading structure/numbering, confirm memo date/conventions, write the final process artifact); the re-run of the 164-assertion suite reported TOTAL 164 / PASS 153 / FAIL 11, with heading-order failures for Issues 9, 12, 14, 17, 18, 20, 21, \"Issue 13 —\" found at -1, a corresponding severity failure, the \"RE matter no\" check for \"Matter No. 25-0341\" failing, and \"1.20x\" not found in the client email, which the agent triaged as mostly artifacts of its own test script.\n\nThe report only partially gestures at this. It does describe the repair itself (quoted below in the rationale) and that a second 164-check suite was later run, but it never reports what that re-run found: there is no mention of the 11 failures (153/164), no heading-order failures, no \"Issue 13 —\" at -1, no \"Matter No. 25-0341\" failure, no \"1.20x\" email failure, and no triage of those failures as test artifacts. A reader of the report would not learn that the post-repair closeout gate produced 11 FAILs out of 164 assertions — precisely the user_consequence this question tracks. The report's closest statements are the repair description: \"The agent inserted the section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document. It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.\" and the verification item: \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.\" The second sentence names the 164-check suite but gives no result for it, and the 265/15 figures describe the earlier pre-repair run, not the final re-run. That is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer what the final full verification re-run reported, so the answer is absent from the report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct on the re-run's reported result; no scoreable content contradicts it either.", "evidence": ["card_0121", "card_0122", "card_0123"]}, {"id": "question_41", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent root-caused and resolved the 11 final verification failures without editing the memo, and what the corrected check confirmed. Per card_0124, card_0125, and card_0126, the answer is: grep showed the Issue 13/13A headings exist in the memo (lines 201/217) and the RE line with the matter number exists (line 11); \"1.20x\" occurs 0 times in the client email (it is sourced in the loan agreement/model while the email carries 1.33x), so that failure was a check-script attribution bug; the \"Issue 13 —\" failure was a double-em-dash bug (searching \"Issue 13 — —\"); the seven ordering failures came from the script assuming numeric 1..22 order instead of the memo's intentional topical grouping; and a corrected regex pass then enumerated the true document order (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 12, 7, 9, 10, 11, 18, 8, 13, 14, 13A, 17, 15, 21, 20, 19, 16, 22), confirming all 23 headings present with severity labels and correct 1.20x/1.33x sourcing — all without modifying the memo.\n\nThe report contains none of this. I searched for \"11\", \"eleven\", \"final\", \"em-dash\", \"Issue 13 — —\", \"1.33x\", \"topical\", \"numeric\", \"grep\", and the document-order list; none appear. The only gestures are the earlier first-run failures, phrased vaguely: \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.\" and \"The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265. The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error.\" This describes a different, earlier run, never names the 11 final failures, gives no root causes (double-dash search, ordering assumption, 1.20x sourcing), and does not say what the corrected check confirmed. The report's \"Defect found and repaired\" item is about the missing Issue 5 body section, a separate issue. A vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question: missing_from_report, score 0.", "evidence": ["card_0124", "card_0125", "card_0126"]}, {"id": "question_42", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks (a) what correction the agent decided on for the Issue 19 summary-table row (change \"LA §6.3(b), (c), (f)\" to \"LA §6.3(c), (f)\", per card_0127) and (b) whether it was applied to the delivered memo (it was not: card_0131 shows the agent closing out with \"All verified\" and final tasks limited to process notes, docx validation, and a final message). The report never mentions Issue 19's table row, the §6.3(b)/(c)/(f) citation, or the decided-but-unapplied correction. The only gesture at the topic is a one-line Medium-severity list that says \"insurance above market\" (in the \"Medium (8)\" findings table), which does not materially answer the question about the specific citation correction or its non-application. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is accurate.", "evidence": ["card_0127", "card_0131"]}, {"id": "question_43", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report only gestures at this topic without answering it. It says the round-trip verification \"The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite,\" and section 5 repeats that \"The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error.\" That is a vague general characterization of the 15 failures. It never identifies the \"RE matter no\" / \"Matter No. 25-0341\" assertion, never names the root cause (the U+00A0 non-breaking space pandoc's markdown-to-docx conversion introduced after \"No.\" in the RE line, as cards 0128 and 0131 show), never describes confirming the nbsp inside the delivered docx by unzipping issue-memorandum.docx and inspecting word/document.xml (\"nbsp before 25-0341? True\", card_0130), and never states the decision to treat it as visually harmless and accept it without changing the memo. The material trace-grounded answer is absent, so this is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0128", "card_0129", "card_0130", "card_0131"]}, {"id": "question_44", "answer": "As its last step before close-out, the agent ran a final integrity check on issue-memorandum.docx in which it \"confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words)\" and wrote process/session-note-02-verification.md before sending a final summary. The pandoc round-trip was part of the verification step: the agent \"round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources,\" and the closing check re-confirmed that the file opens with pandoc. That check confirmed the deliverable is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc, running about 8,230 words.", "quote": "The agent round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report's only treatment of the final check is the close-out item: \"Close-out<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0131–0135<\/span>). The agent wrote <span class=\"ref\">process/session-note-02-verification.md<\/span>, confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words), and sent a final summary to the user.\" That is a correct but partial summary of the card_0134 check (valid zip / well-formed document.xml, opens in pandoc, 8,231 words). But it never answers the explicitly asked \"why\": card_0133 shows the agent deliberately chose the pandoc round-trip because it mirrors how the evaluator will open the file (\"do one final sanity check that the document opens properly with pandoc (as the evaluator will do)\"), and no reason for including pandoc appears anywhere in the report — the word \"evaluator\" does not occur at all. The report also omits the OOXML/zip structural check, the correctly rendered memo header, the 34,555-byte file in place, and the retained process/ artifacts. The material trace-grounded answer, particularly the stated reason for the pandoc round-trip, is absent.", "evidence": ["card_0133", "card_0134", "card_0135"]}], "stages": {"spend": {"census": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "questions": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "answer": {"sessions": 71, "seconds": 25385}, "judge": {"sessions": 44, "seconds": 25499}, "claims": {"sessions": 79, "seconds": 13523}, "recoveries": {"sessions": 5, "seconds": 454}}, "claims": [{"id": "c1", "claim": "The agent produced an internal issue memorandum for partner Katherine Cheng, delivered as issue-memorandum.docx, identifying 23 borrower-unfavorable issues: 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0135 (final summary) states the deliverable is issue-memorandum.docx, a valid OOXML document formatted as an internal memo \"TO Katherine Cheng / FROM Robert Navarro,\" containing 23 borrower-unfavorable issues broken down as 4 Critical, 11 High, 8 Medium. card_0126 shows a scripted check of the pandoc round-trip of the docx that counted 23 issue headings (\"All 23 present\"), and card_0127 confirms all 23 headings carry CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM severity labels. card_0134 shows the final integrity check that the docx is a well-formed zip/OOXML. Together the cards show the memo was produced as stated, with exactly the claimed recipient, file name, issue count, and severity split."}, {"id": "c2", "claim": "The record shows the memorandum was drafted and converted to Word format.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 shows the memorandum being drafted via `cat > process/memo-draft.md` (a full MEMORANDUM with TO/FROM/DATE/RE, 281 lines written). card_0092 shows the pandoc conversion `pandoc process/memo-draft.md -f markdown -t docx ... -o /app/workspace/issue-memorandum.docx` succeeding, with the 32,986-byte issue-memorandum.docx listed (the only failure on that card was the unrelated `file` command not being installed). This matches the claim that the record shows the memorandum was drafted and converted to Word format."}, {"id": "c3", "claim": "The record shows the memorandum was checked with scripted tests and a pandoc round-trip.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0094 shows the pandoc round-trip: `pandoc issue-memorandum.docx -t plain --wrap=none -o process/memo-rt.txt` producing 293 lines of the rendered memo. card_0095 states the docx \"was generated and round-trips without issue\" and that a scripted verification checklist was being run on the final rendered text. card_0096 shows that scripted test: a Python heredoc of hundreds of `ck(name, cond)` assertions over memo-rt.txt (memo conventions, deal metrics, section citations, figures, computed checks). card_0132's verification session note summarizes \"Verification performed (scripted, 265 + 164 assertions)\" and that the deliverable \"round-trips through `pandoc issue-memorandum.docx -t plain`\". … [112 more characters]"}, {"id": "c4", "claim": "The extension-test conclusion was left unresolved.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The trace shows the extension-test conclusion was resolved, not left unresolved. card_0073 shows the agent computing the extension-period DSCRs on an amortizing basis ($3,948,604/$3,378,720 = 1.17x; $4,128,030/$3,378,720 = 1.22x) against the 1.30x test, cross-checking them against the model's own \"Extension DSCR Test (1.30x) FAIL | FAIL\" flags, and concluding \"the Second Extension is mathematically unachievable on the base case.\" card_0107 shows the agent re-verifying the test-date nuance (1.30x measured 30 days before the then-current maturity; for the second extension that falls in the amortizing first extension period) and affirming the memo's conclusion is \"defensible and well-supported … [803 more characters]"}, {"id": "c5", "claim": "The agent flagged a guaranty cross-reference whose correction the record does not show.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace shows the agent did flag a guaranty cross-reference error: card_0067 and card_0077 identify that Guaranty §2.2(b) mis-cites loan §5.4 for financial statements (correct is §6.1) and Guaranty §2.2(a) mis-cites the \"Transfer\" definition from loan §8.2 (correct is §1.1), and card_0079 considers a related guaranty cross-reference mismatch (yield maintenance). These were folded into the memo as drafting issues (card_0084 shows the memo-draft edits, e.g., Issue 13A insertion and drafting-issue handling; card_0132's verification note lists \"Guaranty 2.2(a) cites 'Transfer' from LA §8.2 (should be §1.1); Guaranty 2.2(b) cites financial statements from LA §5.4 (should be §6.1)\" among discrep … [415 more characters]"}, {"id": "c6", "claim": "The agent worked as Robert Navarro, an associate at Ashford, Mead & Tillman LLP, on a review of draft loan and guaranty documents for Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC, the borrower on a $47.6 million acquisition loan from Ironclad National Bank.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 and card_0008 show the source email from Katherine Cheng (Partner, Ashford, Mead & Tillman LLP) to Robert Navarro (rnavarro@ashfordmead.com) assigning him the detailed review of the draft loan agreement and draft guaranty agreement for the Arbor Ridge acquisition financing. card_0008 states the borrower is Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC (the Borrower SPE, acquiring Arbor Ridge Apartments) and the senior loan is $47,600,000 from Ironclad National Bank; card_0011 confirms \"Loan: $47.6M from Ironclad National Bank\" and that the memo is an internal law firm memo from Robert Navarro. The agent acted as Navarro throughout (the memo it produced, per the trace, is signed FROM: Robert Na … [257 more characters]"}, {"id": "c7", "claim": "The matter is Matter No. 25-0341, with borrower SPE Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC, guarantor Marcus Ellison, and lender Ironclad National Bank represented by Prescott Graves LLP.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0008 (decoded email) shows the deal: \"Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC (the Borrower SPE)\", \"$47,600,000 from Ironclad National Bank\", \"Lender's counsel is William Hargrove at Prescott Graves LLP\", \"Marcus Ellison\" as a client contact. card_0011's extracted email details confirm Guarantor: Marcus Ellison, Loan from Ironclad National Bank, and \"Matter No. 25-0341\". card_0014 (loan agreement text) shows Ironclad National Bank as Lender, Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC as Borrower, and \"Prepared by: Prescott Graves LLP\", corroborating that Prescott Graves represents the lender."}, {"id": "c8", "claim": "The subject property is Arbor Ridge Apartments, 312 units, in Brookhaven, Georgia.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0008 (the client email) states: \"Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC ... is acquiring Arbor Ridge Apartments — a 312-unit multifamily complex at 4850 Briarcliff Crossing Drive, Brookhaven, Georgia 30319.\" card_0011 repeats \"Property: Arbor Ridge Apartments, 312 units, 4850 Briarcliff Crossing Drive, Brookhaven, GA 30319.\" The claim's every element (name, 312 units, Brookhaven, Georgia) matches the source."}, {"id": "c9", "claim": "The loan terms cited in the record are: $47.6 million at daily simple SOFR plus 2.65% with a 3.25% floor (5.90% minimum rate), a three-year interest-only initial term with two one-year extensions on a 30-year amortization schedule, closing March 14, 2025, and borrower comments due February 28, 2025.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0011 (the agent's summary of the client email) states every cited term: \"$47.6M from Ironclad National Bank,\" \"daily simple SOFR + 2.65%, SOFR floor 3.25% (minimum effective rate 5.90%),\" \"Initial term: 3 years interest-only; two 1-year extension options with 30-year amortization schedule,\" and \"Closing: March 14, 2025.\" The borrower comments due date is shown in the email itself: cards/full/card_0010.txt (output of the grep over process/email-full.txt, card_0010) contains the partner's line \"we submit the comment letter to Prescott Graves by the February 28 deadline,\" matching the claim's \"borrower comments due February 28, 2025.\" (The February 24 date in card_0011 is the internal memo … [147 more characters]"}, {"id": "c10", "claim": "The assigned deliverable was an internal memorandum to Katherine Cheng due end of day Monday, February 24, 2025.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0008 (the agent's extraction of the full assignment email) records the deliverable as \"Memo: internal law firm memo from Robert Navarro to Katherine Cheng, Matter No. 25-0341, due EOD February 24, 2025,\" matching the claim's addressee, EOD due date, and date. card_0006 shows the email header \"Date: Wed, 19 Feb 2025\" from Katherine Cheng to Robert Navarro, which makes February 24, 2025 a Monday, matching the claim's weekday."}, {"id": "c11", "claim": "For each issue, the memo had to cite the provision, explain the borrower-side problem, assign a severity (Critical, High or Medium) and propose a concrete counter-position.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim states the memo requirements for each issue: cite the provision, explain the borrower-side problem, assign a severity (Critical/High/Medium), and propose a concrete counter-position. card_0075 (turn 78) quotes the governing email instructions verbatim: \"Each issue: (1) cite specific provision and section number; (2) explain why problematic for borrower; (3) assess severity as Critical/High/Medium; (4) provide specific recommended negotiation position or proposed revision\" — matching all four elements. card_0073 (turn 76) corroborates the same four per-issue elements (\"citations, explanation, severity, recommended position\") and the Critical/High/Medium severity scale."}, {"id": "c12", "claim": "Market-standard terms were not to be flagged.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0011 records the instruction from the client email (\"Do not flag standard market provisions,\" plus per-topic \"market standard\" guidance) that the agent treated as its flagging policy; card_0132 shows the decision was carried out — the memo includes section \"XII. Reviewed-but-not-flagged (market-standard list, per Cheng email caution)\" and the process note states the \"Not flagged\" list \"reflects Cheng's instruction not to redline market terms.\" Together the cards show the claim that market-standard terms were not to be flagged."}, {"id": "c13", "claim": "The source materials for the review were the draft loan agreement, the draft guaranty, the Phase I ESA summary, the appraisal summary and the borrower's underwriting model.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim states the review's source materials were the draft loan agreement, draft guaranty, Phase I ESA summary, appraisal summary, and the borrower's underwriting model. card_0002 shows the workspace contained exactly those five documents (draft-loan-agreement.docx, draft-guaranty-agreement.docx, phase-i-esa-summary.docx, appraisal-summary.docx, borrower-underwriting.xlsx) plus the instruction email. card_0004's session note frames the task as reviewing the CRE loan + guaranty, ESA summary, appraisal, and underwriting model. card_0132's verification note lists the pandoc extractions actually drawn from those sources (loan-agreement.md, guaranty.md, esa.md, appraisal.md) plus the underwrit … [109 more characters]"}, {"id": "c14", "claim": "The client priorities, per card_0010.txt, included guaranty recourse beyond standard bad-boy carve-outs and operational flexibility on transfers and property-manager replacement.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 (grep of the email's \"CLIENT CONCERNS / PRIORITY FOCUS AREAS\" section) shows exactly the two claimed priorities: (1) Recourse / Guaranty Exposure — Marcus is \"comfortable with standard 'bad boy' non-recourse carve-out guaranty provisions\" but was alarmed by \"full recourse triggers that go well beyond standard bad boy acts\" (NOI performance, transfer violations, casualty events); and (2) Operational Flexibility — the client needs to \"potentially change property managers if Ridgeline Property Management isn't performing\" and to make changes \"without triggering lender defaults,\" so transfer restrictions and property-management provisions must be scrutinized. card_0011's reasoning summ … [200 more characters]"}, {"id": "c15", "claim": "The client priorities included environmental indemnity exposure from a recognized environmental condition (REC) at the adjacent former dry cleaner at 4870 Briarcliff Crossing Drive.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 shows the client email's \"CLIENT CONCERNS / PRIORITY FOCUS AREAS\" section, whose \"Environmental Risk\" item states the Phase I identified a REC on the adjacent parcel — a former dry-cleaning operation at 4870 Briarcliff Crossing Drive (PCE/TCE) — and that the team needs to ensure the loan agreement's environmental indemnity doesn't saddle the Borrower with off-site/pre-existing contamination liability. This is exactly the claim. card_0011 (the agent's own summary of the email) independently records the same priority: \"environmental (REC on adjacent parcel — former dry cleaner at 4870 Briarcliff Crossing Drive, PCE/TCE; the loan agreement's environmental indemnity should not cover of … [50 more characters]"}, {"id": "c16", "claim": "The client priorities included extension conditions and the interaction of the DSCR covenant, springing cash management and extension tests.", "type": "decision", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 greps the client-concerns section of the email and shows \"Extension Conditions\" as a named priority (\"Look at the financial tests required for extension...\") and \"Financial Covenants and Cash Management\" instructing to \"Review the interplay between the DSCR covenant, the springing cash management trigger, and the extension tests,\" exactly the interaction the claim names. card_0011's reasoning summary independently records the same two items among the client's priority focus areas (extension conditions; DSCR/springing cash management interplay), so the report's passage is a faithful restatement of the client's stated priorities."}, {"id": "c17", "claim": "The task's final state is complete: the deliverable was confirmed to be a well-formed Office document of about 8,230 words that opens with pandoc.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0134 runs the final integrity check on issue-memorandum.docx: the zipfile/ElementTree parse prints \"Real OOXML docx: OK (valid zip, well-formed document.xml)\", pandoc opens it and prints the memo header, and the word count is 8231, i.e. about 8,230 words as claimed. card_0135 confirms the agent's own final handoff treats the deliverable as complete and verified (valid OOXML, opens cleanly with pandoc, ~8,200 words)."}, {"id": "c18", "claim": "As intake, the agent converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 (turn 5) shows the intake listing: the workspace contains four Word documents (phase-i-esa-summary.docx, draft-guaranty-agreement.docx, appraisal-summary.docx, draft-loan-agreement.docx). card_0012 (turn 15) shows the agent converting each of those .docx files to markdown with pandoc (`pandoc <file>.docx -t markdown --wrap=none -o process/<name>.md`), with exit code 0 and wc -l confirming all four markdown outputs (appraisal.md 597 lines, esa.md 227, guaranty.md 429, loan-agreement.md 1033). card_0013's reasoning confirms \"Extracted all documents to Markdown.\" This occurred right after the agent's initial intake steps (workspace listing, email read), matching the claim's \"as intake … [10 more characters]"}, {"id": "c19", "claim": "The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0004 shows the agent's check `python3 -c \"import docx; ...\"` failing with \"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx'\", so python-docx was unavailable. card_0062 shows `pip list | grep -i -E \"openpyxl|xlrd\"` returning nothing (exit code 1, no match), and card_0063 confirms the agent's own note \"No openpyxl\", after which it fell back to parsing the xlsx with the standard library. Together the cards show both openpyxl and python-docx were unavailable, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c20", "claim": "The Excel model was parsed directly from its XML instead of via the unavailable Python libraries.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0062 shows the agent unzipping borrower-underwriting.xlsx with the standard-library zipfile module and grepping pip list for openpyxl/xlrd; the returned namelist (xl/worksheets/sheet1-4.xml, xl/workbook.xml, no sharedStrings.xml) shows no Excel library is installed. card_0063's reasoning states \"No openpyxl. I'll try writing a simple xlsx parser using Python's standard library (xml).\" card_0064 then executes that plan: a python3 script that reads xl/workbook.xml and xl/worksheets/sheetN.xml from the zip and parses rows with xml.etree.ElementTree. Together these show the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML in place of unavailable Python libraries, exactly as claimed."}, {"id": "c21", "claim": "After intake, the work moved to reading the loan agreement.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0011 (end of intake, after the email was read) records the transition decision: \"Next, read the draft loan agreement. It's 79KB of docx — long. Let me convert to markdown with pandoc and split it up.\" card_0012 then converts draft-loan-agreement.docx to process/loan-agreement.md, and card_0014 begins reading it (sed lines 1–180 of loan-agreement.md); the chunk-by-chunk sed reads continue through card_0044, and card_0045 confirms the loan-agreement reading phase ended (\"The loan agreement has been completely mapped out. Next, I'll read the guarantee (guaranty).\"). This matches the report's span of cards 0012–0045 for reading the loan agreement following intake."}, {"id": "c22", "claim": "Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim states tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. Reading process/loan-agreement.md (extracted from draft-loan-agreement.docx in card_0012) produced truncated tool output across many consecutive cards: card_0014 (sed 1,180p) and card_0016 (sed 180,400p) each show \"Warning: truncated output\" plus \"[output truncated to 800 of 10206 chars]\", and card_0018, 0020, 0022, 0024, 0026, 0028, 0030, 0032, 0034, 0036, 0038, and 0040 likewise carry truncation warnings for loan-agreement.md output. card_0017 is the agent's own reasoning explicitly noting \"The middle section is truncated\" for the loan agreement. This is both repeated and specific to the loan agreement, exactly a … [10 more characters]"}, {"id": "c23", "claim": "The agent re-read the loan agreement in line ranges and by section heading to work around the truncation.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "After converting the 1033-line loan agreement to markdown (card_0012), the agent read it in line ranges via sed: '1,180p' (card_0014), '180,400p' (card_0016), and later re-read overlapping/narrower ranges (per the index, '445,650p' card_0020, '650,850p' card_0022, '228,292p' card_0024, '200,228p' card_0026, '399,445p' card_0028, and '449,470p' card_0030). card_0017 shows the agent's reasoning that the middle section was truncated and that it would use grep to find section headings and read the file in parts, and card_0018 shows exactly that grep over Section/ARTICLE headings (returning line-numbered headings like \"35:**[ARTICLE I...\" and \"37:**[Section 1.1...\"); card_0030 pairs a line-range … [132 more characters]"}, {"id": "c24", "claim": "The work then moved to making notes and building a findings log.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0043 (turn 46, within the cited cards 0043-0068 range) shows the agent compiling notes — an extended list of \"Key points of the loan agreement so far\" flagging non-market terms (NOI-decline recourse, casualty cure, environmental indemnity, exit fee, prepayment, extension conditions). Within the same range, card_0068 (turn 71) shows the agent writing process/findings-log.md, a structured findings log (\"Findings log (draft issues) — Arbor Ridge / Matter 25-0341\") with CRITICAL and HIGH issues drawn from the loan agreement, guaranty, ESA, appraisal, and underwriting model. Together these show the work moving to making notes and building a findings log, as claimed."}, {"id": "c25", "claim": "The agent created process/findings-log.md containing 23 numbered items.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0068 shows the agent running `cat > process/findings-log.md` whose content contains 23 numbered items (1-23 across CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM sections, e.g. \"1. NOI-decline full recourse trigger\" through \"23. CM waterfall excess\"), and the echo/ls evidence confirms the file was written. card_0076 shows a later `cat >>` appending a non-numbered \"Guaranty additional facts\" section, so the 23 numbered items remained the numbered content."}, {"id": "c26", "claim": "The findings log included a list of provisions the agent judged market-standard.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0068 shows the agent writing process/findings-log.md, and card_0076 shows a later append to it; both contain a section headed \"## Not flagged (deliberately, per email caution) — market-standard\" that enumerates specific provisions (hard lockbox §5.3(a), 2-mo escrow cushion, reporting cadence, 0.75% orig fee, 18-mo prepay lockout, 5% late charge, etc.), i.e., a list of provisions the agent judged market-standard inside the findings log."}, {"id": "c27", "claim": "The work moved to model and guaranty checks.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cited cards show the work shifting to the financial model and the guaranty. card_0064 parses borrower-underwriting.xlsx (Summary, Cash Flow Projection, Renovation Budget, Returns sheets), i.e. the model. card_0066 reads the model's Cash Flow Projection (sheet2) and appends to findings-log.md a \"Guaranty additional facts\" section (G §2.2/2.3/7.1/8.1/9.5) plus a \"DSCR re-verification\" section cross-checking the model's covenant-compliance flags. card_0076 appends the same guaranty and model DSCR re-verification findings, confirming the model-and-guaranty check phase."}, {"id": "c28", "claim": "The agent appended guaranty facts to the findings log.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0076 shows the agent running `cat >> process/findings-log.md` with a heredoc headed \"## Guaranty additional facts (for Issue 2 / mechanics + assignment)\", appending guaranty details (G §§2.2, 2.3, 3.x, 7.x, 8.1, 9.x) to the findings log; card_0068 shows process/findings-log.md already existed, so this is an append as stated."}, {"id": "c29", "claim": "The agent read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0062 confirms borrower-underwriting.xlsx contains exactly four worksheets. card_0064 shows the agent's script iterating over every row of sheets 1-4 (named by workbook.xml as Summary, Cash Flow Projection, Renovation Budget, Returns) and printing each non-empty row, with exit code 0, i.e. the Summary, Cash Flow, and Returns tabs were read in full (not just grepped or sampled). card_0066 shows the agent subsequently re-dumping the entire Cash Flow sheet (sheet2.xml row by row), reinforcing the full read."}, {"id": "c30", "claim": "The agent re-derived the DSCR arithmetic.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show the agent explicitly re-deriving the DSCR figures rather than just reading them. card_0107 recomputes each ratio from its components: in-place NOI / IO debt service ($3,734,880/$2,808,400 = 1.33x), the first-extension requirement (1.30 × $2,808,400 = $3,650,920 vs model NOI $3,776,676 → 1.345x), and the amortizing extensions ($3,948,604/$3,378,720 = 1.17x). card_0132's session note records these as \"recomputed 3,948,604/3,378,720; 4,128,030/3,378,720,\" and card_0127 re-verifies the same arithmetic against the memo. That is the DSCR arithmetic being re-derived, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c31", "claim": "The work moved to drafting the memorandum.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The transition to memo drafting is directly shown. Up through card_0070 the agent is still cross-referencing source documents (grep over loan-agreement/guaranty/esa). card_0071's reasoning marks the shift: \"All verified. Now I have comprehensive evidence... Now let me write the memo... Let me write the memo markdown now.\" card_0072 then creates the memo by writing process/memo-draft.md (a full law-firm issue memorandum with organized topic sections). The two remaining cited cards, card_0082 and card_0084, are python3 edit passes that refine the same process/memo-draft.md (adding Issue 13A, fixing DSCR/LTV wording, updating the summary table and priorities), both reporting \"edits applied; lin … [129 more characters]"}, {"id": "c32", "claim": "The agent wrote process/memo-draft.md, a draft of about 280 lines covering Sections I–XIII.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 shows the agent writing process/memo-draft.md via a heredoc whose content runs from \"## I. INTRODUCTION AND SCOPE\" through \"## XIII. RECOMMENDED NEGOTIATION PRIORITIES\", and the command's output reports \"wrote 281 lines\" — matching \"about 280 lines\" and Sections I–XIII. card_0073 (the following turn) confirms the draft was in place and the agent proceeded to verify it before building the docx."}, {"id": "c33", "claim": "The agent chose to refine the memo draft through scripted Python edits rather than manual rewriting.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The memo draft was written once (card_0072 via a heredoc), and every subsequent\nrefinement was a scripted python3 edit: card_0082 runs a python3 heredoc that\nreads process/memo-draft.md and does anchored s.replace() rewrites labeled\n\"Refine Issue 1\" and \"Refine Issue 4\"; card_0100 and card_0120 likewise open\nprocess/memo-draft.md in python, replace/insert issue text (e.g. \"Issue 5\ninserted\" with s.replace(anchor, issue5 + anchor) and open(p,'w').write(s)),\nthen regenerate the docx. No manual-rewrite (full-file re-typing) of the memo\noccurs after the initial write, so the decision to refine via scripted Python\nedits is shown in the trace."}, {"id": "c34", "claim": "The scripted edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict, extension LTV and amortization wording, the scope paragraph and dates, the default-rate and exit-fee notes, Phase I timing, and a new Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0082 is the scripted python edit that \"Refine[s] Issue 1\" to add the Schedule 1 NOI conflict (Schedule 1 objective methodology vs Guaranty sole-discretion NOI) and rewrites Issue 4 to state the extension LTV (71.26%/73.80%, LTV ≤ 70% test) and the 30-year-amortization debt-service step-up wording. card_0084 is the follow-up scripted edit that (1) updates the scope paragraph adding the appraisal/Phase I report and site-visit dates, (2) adds the default-rate note on Guaranty §7.2 (Issue 11), (3) clarifies the exit fee at scheduled maturity (Issue 10), (4) fixes the Phase I 180-day AAI / 15-day update timing (Issue 20), and (5) inserts a new \"### Issue 13A — Guaranty: Lender's unilateral r … [156 more characters]"}, {"id": "c35", "claim": "The work moved to converting the memo to Word.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The report passage cites cards 0086–0092 for \"Conversion to Word.\" Within that span, card_0087's reasoning states the next step explicitly: \"Next, convert the markdown to docx with pandoc and apply the reference style,\" i.e. the memo (process/memo-draft.md) to a Word document. card_0088 begins the conversion setup (extracting pandoc's default reference.docx), card_0090 produces a custom reference.docx (Times New Roman, 11pt), and card_0092 runs the conversion itself: pandoc process/memo-draft.md -f markdown -t docx --reference-doc=/tmp/ref-custom.docx -o issue-memorandum.docx, which created issue-memorandum.docx (32986 bytes; only the trailing `file` command was missing). The trace thus show … [210 more characters]"}, {"id": "c36", "claim": "The agent built a custom pandoc reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt) for the Word conversion.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0090 shows the agent printing pandoc's default reference.docx, unzipping it, patching word/styles.xml to replace Cambria with Times New Roman (ascii/hAnsi/cs/eastAsia) and changing w:sz/w:szCs from 24 to 22 half-points (11 pt), then zipping it into /tmp/ref-custom.docx — output confirms \"styles patched\" and the file was created. card_0091 states the plan to create the docx using this custom reference document (Times New Roman), and card_0092 shows the Word conversion actually run with --reference-doc=/tmp/ref-custom.docx, so the document was built and used for the conversion as claimed."}, {"id": "c37", "claim": "The agent converted the markdown memo to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0092 shows the agent running `pandoc process/memo-draft.md -f markdown -t docx --reference-doc=/tmp/ref-custom.docx -o /app/workspace/issue-memorandum.docx`, converting the markdown memo to issue-memorandum.docx with pandoc; the subsequent `ls` confirms the file (32986 bytes) was produced. The trailing `file` command failure (command not found) does not affect the pandoc conversion, which succeeded."}, {"id": "c38", "claim": "The work moved to a verification phase.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0093 marks the transition directly: after the docx is created the agent states \"Now let's verify it\" and plans a full verification pass against the sources, exactly the passage's start point. The cited cards then show sustained verification work: card_0096 and card_0122 run scripted cross-checks of memo facts, figures, and citations against the source documents; card_0132 writes process/session-note-02-verification.md, an explicit verification-phase process note; card_0134 performs the final integrity check (valid zip/docx, pandoc round-trip, deliverable in place). The trace shows the work genuinely moving into a verification phase as claimed."}, {"id": "c39", "claim": "The first verification run comprised 265 checks with 15 failures.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 shows the first scripted verification run over memo-rt.txt printing \"TOTAL 265  PASS 250  FAIL 15\", and its analysis walks through exactly 15 named failures as formatting artifacts. card_0102 shows a smaller follow-up re-check of those 15 failures, confirming it was a second pass, not the first run. card_0132's session note records \"Verification performed (scripted, 265 + 164 assertions)\", corroborating that the 265-check run was the initial verification. The claim's \"265 checks with 15 failures\" matches the card evidence exactly."}, {"id": "c40", "claim": "The agent judged the 15 first-run failures to be formatting differences or checker errors.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 shows the first verification run ending \"TOTAL 265 PASS 250 FAIL 15\" and listing the 15 FAIL lines (e.g., figure 1.25x in source, LA: IR cap 3.50 strike A-rated, ESA: Phase II option 15-25k). card_0097 shows the agent working through those same 15 failures and judging them as formatting differences or checker errors — \"Most of the 'failures' are formatting mismatches (the documents write '1.25 to 1.00' instead of '1.25x'...)\", \"The check's regex was too strict\", quote/apostrophe escaping in the markdown, and the intent to \"confirm that all 15 'failures' are just formatting artifacts.\" The decision attributed in the claim is exactly what the trace records."}, {"id": "c41", "claim": "The agent re-ran the verification suite after dismissing the first run's failures.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 shows the first run of the scripted verification suite (a python3 assertion script over the memo and sources). card_0097 shows the agent dismissing that run's 15 failures as formatting artifacts (\"Most of the 'failures' are formatting mismatches... let me quickly verify 12–14 and confirm that all 15 'failures' are just formatting artifacts\"). After subsequent memo edits, card_0121 explicitly states the intent to \"re-run the full verification suite... one more time\", and card_0122 executes that re-run, reporting \"TOTAL 164 PASS 153 FAIL 11\". This matches the claim that the agent re-ran the verification suite after dismissing the first run's failures."}, {"id": "c42", "claim": "The agent later ran a second verification suite of 164 checks.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 (turn 99) shows the first scripted verification suite printing \"TOTAL 265 PASS 250 FAIL 15\". card_0121 (turn 124) shows the agent deciding to re-run the full verification suite after restoring Issue 5. card_0122 (turn 125) shows a second, distinct check script whose output is \"TOTAL 164 PASS 153 FAIL 11\" — i.e., a second verification suite of 164 checks run later in the session, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c43", "claim": "The agent round-tripped the Word file to plain text as part of verification.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0094 shows the agent running `pandoc issue-memorandum.docx -t plain --wrap=none -o process/memo-rt.txt`, a successful Word-to-plain-text round-trip (293 lines of memo text returned). card_0093's reasoning states this was done as verification (\"I'll re-open it with pandoc... round-trip conversion back to markdown/text\"), and card_0102 round-trips the same docx to plain text again to run the fact-check verification pass."}, {"id": "c44", "claim": "The scripted checks verified section citations, figures and quoted phrases in the memo against the extracted sources.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The report passage says the agent \"ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources.\" The cards show exactly this. card_0096 (turn 99) runs a Python script loading memo-rt.txt plus the extracted sources (loan-agreement.md, guaranty.md, esa.md, appraisal.md, email-full.txt) and asserts: (a) every LA/Guaranty section cited in the memo exists in the source doc (citation check), and (b) each memo figure (NOI, DS, fees, reserves, DSCR/LTV/DY, dates, project numbers) is present in both the memo and its source file (figure check). card_0122 (turn 125) runs another Python check whose \"key source facts\" list includes verbatim quoted phrases (\"without … [559 more characters]"}, {"id": "c45", "claim": "A defect was found: the summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0112 greps \"^Issue \" headings in the rendered memo (memo-rt.txt) and the list skips from Issue 4 (line 103) straight to Issue 6 (line 121), with no Issue 5 body heading. card_0114's grep for \"Issue 5|Major-casualty\" in the rendered memo returns only line 36, the summary-table row \"5  Major-casualty full-recourse trigger ... LA §8.2(a), §12.1(d) ... High\" — i.e., Issue 5 is present in the summary table but absent from the body. card_0115 and card_0116 are the agent's own conclusions at that moment: \"The body of Issue 5 is missing!\" and \"Issue 5 (Major Casualty) is missing from the main text — it's only included in the summary table.\" Both elements of the claim (listed in the summary tabl … [64 more characters]"}, {"id": "c46", "claim": "The agent inserted the missing Issue 5 section before Section IV and restored the 23 headings.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace shows the claimed recovery exactly as stated. card_0073 (and card_0113/card_0115) show the agent noticing the Issue 5 (major-casualty) body section was missing from the memo — it appeared only in the summary table. card_0116 greps the draft before the fix and lists 22 \"### Issue\" headings with Issue 5 absent. card_0120 then runs a Python edit that asserts the anchor \"## IV. TRANSFERS / CHANGE OF CONTROL\" and replaces it with the full Issue 5 section followed by that anchor, i.e. inserting the missing Issue 5 section immediately before Section IV; the same command regenerates the docx and reports \"Issue 5 inserted\", \"23\" Issue headings, and \"Issue 5 — Major-casualty full-recourse tr … [330 more characters]"}, {"id": "c47", "claim": "The agent re-checked the document after inserting the missing section.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0120 shows the agent inserting the missing \"Issue 5\" section into memo-draft.md, then in the same command regenerating issue-memorandum.docx, re-extracting it to memo-rt.txt, and re-checking it (grep -c \"^Issue \" returned 23 and grep -n \"^Issue 5\" showed the new section at line 119). card_0121 records the intent to re-run the full verification suite, and card_0122 shows that full 164-check verification being run against the re-extracted document after the insertion."}, {"id": "c48", "claim": "The agent moved Issue 13A into its correct position in the memo.", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the agent \"moved Issue 13A into its correct position in the memo.\" The trace shows the opposite: 13A was never repositioned. card_0084 shows the only edit touching 13A's placement — it inserted the new 13A subsection \"after Issue 14's block, before Issue 17,\" so the body order was 13, 14, 13A, 17. card_0111 records the agent noticing the mismatch (table lists 13, 13A, 14; body has 13, 14, 13A) and deciding \"13A should be moved to between 13 and 14. Let me fix the ordering.\" But no card executes that move: card_0116 greps the draft headings and shows 13A still at line 201, after Issue 14 (line 193), and card_0126's final structural check prints the delivered memo's document ord … [216 more characters]"}, {"id": "c49", "claim": "The work moved to close-out.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Cards 0131-0135, the exact range the report cites, are the terminal close-out phase of the trace. card_0131 (turn 134) states \"All verified. Final tasks: 1. Finalize process notes ... 2. Final validation of the docx file ... 3. Clean final message.\" card_0132 writes the final process note process/session-note-02-verification.md (\"session end\"). card_0134 runs the \"Final integrity check\" (valid OOXML docx, pandoc round-trip, word count, deliverable in place) and it passes. card_0135 (turn 138, the last turn) delivers the final handoff summary. This is precisely the work moving to close-out as claimed."}, {"id": "c50", "claim": "During close-out the agent wrote process/session-note-02-verification.md.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0132 (turn 135) shows the agent running `cat > process/session-note-02-verification.md << 'EOF' ...` writing the full \"Process note — 02 (verification & outcome)\" content, dated 2026-10-05 session end, i.e. during close-out; card_0133 confirms the subsequent final integrity check. The file the report names is exactly the one written."}, {"id": "c51", "claim": "The agent sent a final summary to the user.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0135 is the final turn (138) and contains an assistant message that is the final summary sent to the user: it states \"The deliverable is complete and verified. Here's the handoff\" and lays out the deliverable, its 23 issues, verification results, and an offer of next steps. card_0131's plan explicitly included \"3. Clean final message,\" and that message is delivered at the end of the trajectory."}, {"id": "c52", "claim": "The agent's own reasoning (card_0077) noted that the first extension DSCR test is probably measured on interest-only debt service.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0077's reasoning explicitly works through this point: \"the loan at that point is still IO (amortization begins on the effective date of the extension). If the test uses IO debt service: 3,776,676/2,808,400 = 1.345x ≥ 1.30x — passes. But the model states FAIL. Hmm — but wait ... This suggests the model calculated DSCR at extension-period amortizing debt service.\" The agent therefore noted exactly that the first extension DSCR test is probably (at least could plausibly be) measured on interest-only debt service, with its pass/fail turning on which debt service applies."}, {"id": "c53", "claim": "On that basis, the Year 3 DSCR figure of about 1.345x would pass the 1.30x test.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0107 shows the agent computing the first extension's DSCR test: 1.30x of interest-only debt service requires NOI >= $3,650,920; Year 3 (2027-2028) model NOI is $3,776,676, so DSCR = 3,776,676/2,808,400 = 1.345x, and concludes the 1.30x test is met. card_0073 independently reaches the same result: the TTM measurement period ending ~30 days before 3/14/2028 falls in the initial IO term, so DS = $2,808,400, TTM NOI = model Year 3 ($3,776,676), DSCR = 1.345x >= 1.30 → PASS (base case). The claim that \"the Year 3 DSCR figure of about 1.345x would pass the 1.30x test\" matches this work exactly."}, {"id": "c54", "claim": "The Year 3 debt yield of 7.93% would still fail the 8.00% test.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0024 quotes loan agreement §2.6(iii): the extension condition requires \"The Debt Yield shall be not less than 8.00%, as of the date that is thirty (30) days prior to the then-current Maturity Date,\" establishing the 8.00% test. card_0077 shows the model arithmetic: Y3 NOI 3,776,676 / 47,600,000 = 7.93%, and the agent's analysis concludes \"first extension DY test (8.00%) ... TTM NOI ≈ Y3 = 3,776,676 → DY 7.93% < 8.00% → fails in the base case,\" restated as \"the extension debt yield test fails in the base case\" for Y3. Together the cards show the Year 3 debt yield of 7.93% against the 8.00% test, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c55", "claim": "The memo's Issue 4 is titled as structurally unachievable and concludes that the effective term is three years.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0112 greps the rendered final memo (process/memo-rt.txt) and shows the heading \"Issue 4 — Extension conditions are structurally unachievable (CRITICAL)\", matching the claim that Issue 4 is titled as structurally unachievable. card_0114 shows the full Issue 4 section of the final memo, whose concluding sentence is \"The realistic result is that, even performing exactly to the base case Marcus underwrote, the borrower may be unable to exercise the extensions and will be forced into a refinancing or sale at the end of year three\" — i.e., the issue concludes the extensions are unattainable and the effective term is three years, exactly as the claim states (earlier analysis cards such as card … [73 more characters]"}, {"id": "c56", "claim": "The extension conclusion is open: the records read do not show that the first-extension point was reconciled in the final text, and the conclusion depends on it.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim asserts the records do not show the first-extension point being reconciled in the final text. The cards show the opposite. card_0107 (turn 110) captures the agent explicitly working through the first-extension question: the first extension's 1.30x DSCR test is measured ~30 days before 3/14/2028, i.e., during the interest-only period, so it could pass (1.34x on IO debt service), while the LTV 70% test still blocks the first extension. The agent then re-reads the final Issue 4 text and closes the point: \"It doesn't falsely claim the first extension's DSCR fails; it correctly anchors to the second extension. And the LTV paragraph separately establishes that the LTV test blocks both ex … [811 more characters]"}, {"id": "c57", "claim": "The first draft of Issue 13 cited \"Guaranty §6.1(d)\" for reporting.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 is the command that first writes process/memo-draft.md (the first draft of the memorandum), and its Issue 13 entry contains the provision line \"LA §7.3, §10.1(l); Guaranty §3.1–3.3, §6.1(d) (reporting)\" — i.e., the draft cited \"Guaranty §6.1(d)\" for reporting, exactly as the claim states. card_0073 confirms this was the draft's text, noting \"Issue 13 cites 'Guaranty §6.1(d) (reporting)' — this is wrong... the reporting obligation is in §3.4\" and listing the fix to §3.4, showing the §6.1(d) citation existed in the first draft and was later corrected."}, {"id": "c58", "claim": "The agent noted (card_0073) that §6.1 is the cooperation section, that the reporting obligation sits in §3.4, and it listed the fix.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0073 (turn 76) shows the agent's verification notes stating verbatim that Issue 13's citation \"Guaranty §6.1(d) (reporting)\" is wrong because \"Guaranty §6.1 is 'Cooperation,' and the reporting obligation is in §3.4. Fix it.\" This matches the claim exactly: the agent identified §6.1 as the cooperation section, placed the reporting obligation in §3.4, and listed the fix."}, {"id": "c59", "claim": "No later card shows the guaranty cross-reference correction applied, so it is not confirmed as corrected.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The guaranty cross-reference errors (Guaranty \"Transfer\" definition citing LA §8.2 instead of §1.1; Guaranty §2.2(b) citing LA §5.4 instead of §6.1 for financial statements) are identified in card_0067 and card_0077, which show only analysis and flagging. A survey of all 135 cards shows every write operation (cat > or python open-for-write) targets only process notes, findings-log, memo-draft.md, memo-rt.txt, and issue-memorandum.docx — no card ever writes to draft-guaranty-agreement.docx or process/guaranty.md, which are only read. The final verification cards (card_0132, card_0135) still list the drafting errors among the memo's flagged issues (\"wrong ESA project number, 'Transfer' and fin … [223 more characters]"}, {"id": "c60", "claim": "The later checks confirmed that cited sections exist, which would not by itself catch a wrong subsection.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 shows the verification script whose citation checks only assert that each cited\nsection number exists in the source documents (\"LA §X.X exists in loan agreement\" /\n\"Guaranty §X.X exists in guaranty\" via regex over the source text), which is an existence\ncheck and cannot catch a citation pointing to the wrong subsection. card_0132 records the\nsame later verification (\"Every LA/Guaranty section cited in the memo exists in the source\ndocx\") and, in its discrepancy list, shows that the check ran alongside known wrong\nsubsections that it did not catch on their own (Guaranty 2.2(a) cites LA §8.2 \"should be\n§1.1\"; 2.2(b) cites LA §5.4 \"should be §6.1\"). card_0135 restates the final verifi … [208 more characters]"}, {"id": "c61", "claim": "The memo refers to Georgia provisions (O.C.G.A. §10-7-3, material alteration; and §44-14-161, anti-deficiency) and describes certain terms as standard.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0072 writes the memo (process/memo-draft.md), which states that the Guaranty's §2.3 \"waives every standard suretyship defense — ... the right to raise a material-alteration defense under O.C.G.A. § 10-7-3, the anti-deficiency protections under O.C.G.A. § 44-14-161,\" matching the claim's exact statute-to-doctrine mapping. The same memo describes terms as standard elsewhere (\"a broad waiver package is common in a true bad-boy carve-out guaranty\"; Section XII lists reviewed terms that \"are within the range of customary terms\"). card_0077 shows the drafting reasoning that the \"waiver of the O.C.G.A. § 10-7-3 material alteration defense and anti-deficiency is standard in a CRE guaranty,\" con … [76 more characters]"}, {"id": "c62", "claim": "The statute references are not sourced in the record: the records contain no statute text for these citations, and the verification checked citations only against the loan documents and model.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 (the only workspace listing) shows the record consists solely of the loan documents and deal materials (draft-loan-agreement.docx, draft-guaranty-agreement.docx, borrower-underwriting.xlsx, appraisal-summary.docx, phase-i-esa-summary.docx, cheng-to-navarro-email.eml) — there is no statute text or code book in the record, and no card anywhere shows statute text being fetched or read (the only statute-adjacent text found is \"No statute of limitations\" quoted from the guaranty itself in card_0127/card_0122). The scripted verification passes in card_0096, card_0122, and card_0132 load exactly the loan-agreement, guaranty, ESA, appraisal, and email extractions (plus the underwriting mod … [686 more characters]"}, {"id": "c63", "claim": "Tool output was repeatedly truncated, so the agent re-read sections of the loan agreement in ranges.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Multiple loan-agreement reads show \"Warning: truncated output\" / \"[output truncated to 800 of N chars]\" markers: card_0014 (sed -n '1,180p'), card_0016 (sed -n '180,400p'), card_0020 (sed -n '445,650p'), card_0022 (sed -n '650,850p'), card_0028 (sed -n '399,445p'), card_0032. The agent explicitly attributes the follow-up to truncation: card_0017's reasoning says \"The middle section is truncated. Let me check the remaining parts of the loan agreement ... let me look at lines 240-420\". Afterwards the agent re-reads the agreement in overlapping ranges: card_0024 (sed -n '228,292p'), card_0026 (sed -n '200,228p'), card_0028 (sed -n '399,445p'), plus card_0030/card_0032 targeting the gaps. This m … [108 more characters]"}, {"id": "c64", "claim": "The records do not show a single complete pass over the full loan agreement; coverage of the full agreement rests on those re-reads.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The loan agreement was extracted to process/loan-agreement.md as a 1033-line file (card_0012's wc -l, and the agent's own notes in card_0013/card_0015: \"1033 lines. Let's go through it chunk by chunk\"). No card contains a command that reads the full file in a single pass; the only full-file touch without a line range is card_0018, a grep of section headings (not a pass over content). Full coverage was assembled from many chunked re-reads: card_0014 (sed 1-180), card_0016 (180-400), card_0020 (445-650), card_0022 (650-850), plus overlapping re-reads card_0024/0026/0028/0030/0032/0034/0036/0040 (e.g., 55-140, 200-228, 399-445, 540-700, 353-357, 501-535), card_0038 (awk over Section 10.1), card … [389 more characters]"}, {"id": "c65", "claim": "The model's DSCR row has five values against six labelled columns, and the Returns tab labels exits as Years 3–5.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0075 records the agent reading the model's KEY METRICS tab and noticing the DSCR row carries only 5 values (\"1.20x | 1.29x | 1.34x | 1.17x | 1.22x\") against six labelled columns (Year 0, Year 1, Year 2, Year 3, Year 4/Ext 1, Year 5/Ext 2), matching the first half of the claim. card_0073 shows the Returns tab listing \"Projected NOI at Exit\" for \"Year 3 Exit / Year 4 Exit / Year 5 Exit\", i.e. the exits are labelled Years 3-5, matching the second half of the claim."}, {"id": "c66", "claim": "The agent relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 shows the agent parsing borrower-underwriting.xlsx and enumerating its sheets, including \"Returns\" (sheet 4). card_0075 shows the agent reading the model's own \"COVENANT COMPLIANCE\" block (DSCR Covenant 1.25x BELOW/BELLOW, Extension DSCR Test 1.30x FAIL/FAIL, Cash Management Trigger AT RISK) and the Returns tab's \"Projected NOI at Exit\" figures ($3,776,676 Yr3, $3,948,604 Yr4, $4,128,030 Yr5), then anchoring its extension-period DSCR analysis (1.17x/1.22x) directly on those two model blocks. This matches the claim that the agent relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI."}, {"id": "c67", "claim": "The memo quotes returns figures from the Returns tab: Year 3 at 1.04x and 1.3%; Year 5 at 1.37x and 8.4%; and 8.1% and 1.36x at the lender-reserve level.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 confirms the underwriting model's Returns tab holds exactly these values: Year-3 exit equity multiple 1.04x / levered IRR 1.3%, Year-5 exit 1.37x / 8.4%, and 8% preferred return not achieved in any base case exit. card_0127 shows the memo text quoting those figures from the Returns tab, plus the lender-reserve level numbers: the memo's Note on Capital Reserves states funding at the Lender's $300/unit level reduces the adjusted Year-5 IRR from 8.4% to 8.1% and the equity multiple from 1.37x to 1.36x, matching the xlsx \"Adjusted Levered IRR (at lender escrow level)\" 8.1% and \"Adjusted Equity Multiple (at lender escrow level)\" 1.36x. card_0132's session note records that the final ver … [109 more characters]"}, {"id": "c68", "claim": "The Year 3 profit figure of $705,499 does not reconcile with the total-return and equity figures shown on the same tab, so those returns figures carry the same uncertainty.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 dumps the full Returns tab (sheet 4) of borrower-underwriting.xlsx. Its Year 3 Exit column shows Total Equity Invested $18,750,000, Total Return (Cumul. Cash Flow + Equity Proceeds) $18,704,476, Profit (Loss) $705,499, and Equity Multiple 1.04x. These do not reconcile: Total Return minus Total Equity Invested is -$45,524, not the $705,499 profit line (the $705,499 actually equals Net Equity Proceeds $19,455,499 minus Total Equity Invested, i.e., it omits the -$751,023 cumulative operating cash flows that the Total Return row includes). Consistently, the 1.04x equity multiple derives from proceeds, while Total Return / invested equity is ~0.998x. card_0085 confirms the agent read th … [167 more characters]"}, {"id": "c69", "claim": "The records do not show whether the 18-month prepayment lockout (LA §2.5) would make a mandatory casualty or condemnation prepayment during the lockout an Event of Default; this point was not checked.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim states the records do not show the 18-month prepayment lockout (LA §2.5) vs. mandatory casualty/condemnation prepayment EOD point being checked, and that it was \"not checked.\" The trace shows it was checked. card_0043's findings note \"Section 2.5 Prepayment — 18-month lockout, prepayment is an Event of Default (EoD).\" card_0067 describes the §2.5 lockout EOD and flags that fees \"should probably be carved out for mandatory prepayments from casualty or condemnation proceeds.\" card_0077 contains an explicit issue: \"Prepayment lockout EOD on mandatory prepayments (LA §2.5(a), Article XII) — no carve-out for casualty/condemnation/tax/insurance paydowns; any prepayment during the 18-mont … [175 more characters]"}, {"id": "c70", "claim": "The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265, which the agent attributed to formatting or checker error.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0096 shows the agent running its first scripted verification suite (the python3 check harness) and it printing \"TOTAL 265  PASS 250  FAIL 15\", i.e. 15 failures out of 265 checks as the claim states. card_0097 shows the agent's immediate analysis attributing those failures to formatting or checker-side error: e.g. the LA writes \"1.25 to 1.00\" instead of \"1.25x\" (\"the check's regex was too strict\"), the memo uses \"$42M\" shorthand vs \"$42,000,000\", and escaped quotes/dollar signs in the pandoc markdown caused the search strings to miss. card_0101 then confirms the re-run targeted \"all 15 failures\" with corrected source-format expectations."}, {"id": "c71", "claim": "The record does not confirm that the origination-fee dollar amount ($357,000) was added to the memo, although the agent said it would consider this (card_0107); the memo states the fee as a percentage.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0107 (turn 110, agent reasoning) shows the agent explicitly considering adding the $357,000 origination-fee dollar amount to the notes (\"for completeness let me add the dollar amount for the origination fee\") and then deciding not to (\"the notes don't need every dollar amount ... So these aren't a problem\"), so the dollar amount was not added. card_0108 and card_0110 dump the final memo plain text (lines 120-293 of process/memo-rt.txt) and contain no \"$357,000\" and no origination-fee dollar figure, consistent with the memo stating the fee only as the 0.75% percentage (card_0104 greps the memo for \"0.75% origination\")."}, {"id": "c72", "claim": "The process note's severity header reads \"High 10\" but lists 11 issues.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0132 shows the process note (session-note-02-verification.md) being written with the line \"Severity counts: Critical 4 (1,2,3,4); High 10 (5,6,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,13A,14 — 11 total); Medium 8 (...)\". The header indeed reads \"High 10\" while listing 11 issues (5,6,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,13A,14), matching the claim exactly."}, {"id": "c73", "claim": "The final count of 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium issues matches the list.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0112 shows the final memo's issue headings with severity labels: Issues 1-4 (CRITICAL), Issues 6-14 plus 13A (HIGH), Issues 15-22 (MEDIUM). Issue 5 was initially missing (noted in card_0113) and was then inserted by card_0120's command as \"Issue 5 — Major-casualty full-recourse trigger (HIGH)\", confirmed present in the final docx by card_0114 (summary table row \"5 Major-casualty full-recourse trigger ... High\"). Adding it yields exactly 4 Critical, 11 High (6,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,13A,14,5), and 8 Medium, and card_0127 confirms all 23 issues exist with severity labels in the final document. The claimed final count matches the list."}, {"id": "c74", "claim": "None of the work described in this section is a legal review by a lawyer.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim disclaims that the described work is a legal review by a lawyer. The cards show the work was entirely mechanical agent activity: card_0004 shows the agent planning and starting document review (pandoc/python extraction of the loan/guaranty/ESA/appraisal files), and card_0132 shows the deliverable (issue-memorandum.docx) and all verification done by scripted assertions the agent ran itself. No card shows any lawyer performing a legal review; the work is an automated document analysis, which matches the claim's statement that it is not a legal review by a lawyer."}], "recoveries": [{"event": "event_002", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0004 is the failure: `python3 -c \"import docx\"` returned ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx' while probing tooling. The agent never re-ran the docx import. Instead, in card_0005's reasoning it explicitly pivoted (\"python-docx isn't there, but pandoc works for reading... Pandoc is simpler\"), and card_0006's next attempt at extracting the document content uses pandoc conversion rather than python-docx. When pandoc's .eml output was still quoted-printable, card_0008 changed approach again, decoding with python3's quopri. Each retry used a different tool/approach relevant to the reported missing module, not a repetition of the failed command."}, {"event": "event_004", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0006 is the failure: pandoc conversion of cheng-to-navarro-email.eml returned output truncated to 800 of 10205 chars, cutting off the email's \"CLIENT CONCERNS\" guidance. Rather than re-running pandoc, card_0008 takes a different approach to the same goal: a python3 script that splits the raw .eml, decodes the quoted-printable body with quopri, and writes the full text to process/email-full.txt (bypassing the truncated stdout path). card_0010 then greps the saved file for the missing \"CLIENT CONCERNS\" section and retrieves it in full, confirming the change fixed the cause of the failure."}, {"event": "event_013", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0062 is the failing probe: a zipfile namelist of borrower-underwriting.xlsx plus `pip list | grep openpyxl|xlrd`, which exited code 1 and showed no spreadsheet library installed (the namelist itself listed four worksheets). card_0063 is the agent's stated reaction — \"No openpyxl. I'll try writing a simple xlsx parser using Python's standard library (xml)\" — and card_0064 is the next attempt, which changes approach: instead of relying on openpyxl/xlrd, it reads the xlsx zip directly and parses xl/workbook.xml and the sheet XMLs with the stdlib xml.etree parser, and it succeeds (output code 0, sheet contents printed). The retry changed its method in a way directly responsive to the report … [61 more characters]"}, {"event": "event_036", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0088 is the failure: the reference-docx generation command (pandoc default reference.docx, unzip, patch word/styles.xml to Times New Roman 11pt, rezip) was rejected by the sandbox because its \"rm -rf refx\" tripped the guard (\"rm -f style commands are not permitted\"). The agent's next attempt, card_0090, re-runs the same docx build but removes the rm entirely, uses a fresh temp directory (refx2) with mkdir -p, and unzips with -oq (overwrite) instead of cleaning a stale directory — a change directly addressing the reported cause. The retry succeeds (styles.patched, /tmp/ref-custom.docx produced), and card_0092 then uses that reference doc to build issue-memorandum.docx."}, {"event": "event_040", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0092 is the failing conversion command: pandoc produced issue-memorandum.docx and ls -la succeeded, but the trailing verification step `file issue-memorandum.docx` exited 127 with \"file: command not found\". In card_0093 the agent states it will verify by re-opening the docx with pandoc, and card_0094 does exactly that: `pandoc issue-memorandum.docx -t plain --wrap=none -o process/memo-rt.txt` plus wc/head/grep checks, which succeed (exit 0, 293 lines of expected memo content). The next attempt at the same verification goal changed the approach — replacing the missing `file` binary check with a pandoc round-trip read — rather than re-running the same failing command."}]}, "branches": [], "reports": {"html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Arbor Ridge loan-document issue memorandum: report on the agent's session<\/title>\n<style>\n  body { font-family: Georgia, \"Times New Roman\", serif; line-height: 1.5; color: #1d1d1d; max-width: 860px; margin: 2em auto; padding: 0 1.2em; }\n  h1 { font-size: 1.7em; border-bottom: 2px solid #333; padding-bottom: 0.3em; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.25em; margin-top: 1.8em; border-bottom: 1px solid #bbb; padding-bottom: 0.2em; }\n  .lede { font-size: 1.05em; background: #f4f4f1; padding: 0.9em 1.1em; border-left: 4px solid #4a6fa5; }\n  .note { font-size: 0.92em; color: #444; }\n  .ref { color: #555; font-family: \"Courier New\", monospace; font-size: 0.88em; }\n  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1em 0; width: 100%; font-size: 0.95em; }\n  th, td { border: 1px solid #bbb; padding: 0.4em 0.6em; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }\n  th { background: #eceae4; }\n  figure { margin: 1.5em 0; }\n  figcaption { font-size: 0.92em; color: #333; margin-top: 0.5em; }\n  svg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n<\/style>\n<\/head>\n<body>\n\n<h1>Arbor Ridge loan-document issue memorandum: report on the agent's session<\/h1>\n\n<p class=\"lede\">The agent worked as Robert Navarro, an associate at Ashford, Mead &amp; Tillman LLP, on a review of draft loan and guaranty documents for Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC, the borrower on a $47.6 million acquisition loan from Ironclad National Bank. It produced an internal issue memorandum for partner Katherine Cheng (<span class=\"ref\">issue-memorandum.docx<\/span>) identifying 23 borrower-unfavorable issues: 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium. The record shows the memorandum was drafted, converted to Word format and checked with scripted tests and a pandoc round-trip. Several points are unresolved, including the extension-test conclusion and a guaranty cross-reference the agent flagged but whose correction the record does not show.<\/p>\n\n<h2>1. The assignment<\/h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Parties and matter.<\/strong> Matter No. 25-0341. Borrower SPE: Blackthorn Peachtree Holdings LLC. Guarantor: Marcus Ellison. Lender: Ironclad National Bank, represented by Prescott Graves LLP. Property: Arbor Ridge Apartments, 312 units, Brookhaven, Georgia.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Loan terms cited in the record.<\/strong> $47.6 million; daily simple SOFR plus 2.65% with a 3.25% floor (5.90% minimum rate); three-year interest-only initial term with two one-year extensions on a 30-year amortization schedule; closing March 14, 2025; borrower comments due February 28, 2025.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Deliverable.<\/strong> An internal memorandum to Katherine Cheng, due end of day Monday, February 24, 2025. For each issue the memo had to cite the provision, explain the borrower-side problem, assign a severity (Critical, High or Medium) and propose a concrete counter-position. Market-standard terms were not to be flagged. Sources were the draft loan agreement, the draft guaranty, the Phase I ESA summary, the appraisal summary and the borrower's underwriting model.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Client priorities<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards/full/card_0010.txt<\/span>): guaranty recourse beyond standard bad-boy carve-outs; operational flexibility on transfers and property-manager replacement; environmental indemnity exposure from a recognized environmental condition (REC) at the adjacent former dry cleaner at 4870 Briarcliff Crossing Drive; extension conditions; and the interaction of the DSCR covenant, springing cash management and extension tests.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>2. What the agent did, in order<\/h2>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Intake<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0004–0011<\/span>). The agent read the assignment email and the client-priority notes, then converted the Word documents to markdown with pandoc. The Python openpyxl and python-docx libraries were unavailable, so the Excel model was parsed directly from its XML.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Reading the loan agreement<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0012–0045<\/span>). Tool output for the loan agreement was repeatedly truncated. The agent re-read it in line ranges and by section heading.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Notes and findings log<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0043, 0068<\/span>). The agent recorded first-pass issue notes, then created <span class=\"ref\">process/findings-log.md<\/span> with 23 numbered items and a list of provisions it judged market-standard.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Model and guaranty checks<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0064, 0066, 0076<\/span>). The agent read the model's Summary, Cash Flow and Returns tabs in full, appended guaranty facts to the findings log, and re-derived the DSCR arithmetic.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Drafting<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0072, 0082, 0084<\/span>). The agent wrote <span class=\"ref\">process/memo-draft.md<\/span> (about 280 lines, Sections I–XIII), then refined it through scripted Python edits. The edits covered the Schedule 1 NOI conflict, extension LTV and amortization wording, the scope paragraph and dates, the default-rate and exit-fee notes, Phase I timing, and a new Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Conversion to Word<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0086–0092<\/span>). The agent built a custom reference document (Times New Roman, 11 pt) and converted the markdown to <span class=\"ref\">issue-memorandum.docx<\/span> with pandoc.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Verification<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0093–0107, 0122, 0126–0134<\/span>). The agent round-tripped the Word file to plain text and ran scripted checks of section citations, figures and quoted phrases against the extracted sources. The first run had 265 checks and 15 failures. The agent judged these to be formatting differences or checker errors and re-ran the suite, and it later ran a second suite of 164 checks.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Defect found and repaired<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0111–0129<\/span>). The summary table listed Issue 5 (major-casualty recourse trigger), but the rendered memo had no body section for it. The agent inserted the section before Section IV, restored the 23 headings, and re-checked the document. It also moved Issue 13A into its correct position.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Close-out<\/strong> (<span class=\"ref\">cards 0131–0135<\/span>). The agent wrote <span class=\"ref\">process/session-note-02-verification.md<\/span>, confirmed the file is a well-formed Office document that opens with pandoc (about 8,230 words), and sent a final summary to the user.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h2>3. What the memorandum concludes<\/h2>\n<p>The memorandum groups the 23 issues by topic and gives each one a provision citation, an explanation, a severity and a recommended position. Its headline conclusion is that the draft is effectively full recourse. The principal findings are:<\/p>\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Severity<\/th><th>Issues and subject matter (as stated in the record)<\/th><\/tr>\n  <\/thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Critical (4)<\/td><td>(1) NOI-decline full-recourse trigger: LA §8.2(b) and Guaranty §2.2(b). Recourse follows if NOI falls 20% below the underwritten $3,734,880 (below $2,987,904) for two consecutive quarters. The memo also flags a conflict with the Schedule 1 NOI methodology. (2) Automatic full-recourse mechanics with no contest right (Guaranty §§2.2–2.3). (3) Unlimited, perpetual environmental indemnity that could reach the off-site dry-cleaner REC (LA §§9.1, 9.3; Guaranty Art. 5). (4) Extension conditions the model shows failing: the 70% LTV test fails at opening (47.6M ÷ 66.8M = 71.26%), and the 1.30x DSCR test fails in the extension periods (about 1.17x and 1.22x once amortization applies).<\/td><\/tr>\n    <tr><td>High (11)<\/td><td>Major-casualty recourse trigger (Issue 5); prohibited-transfer full-recourse trigger (Issue 6); springing cash-management trap (Issue 7); assignment without notice or consent (Issue 8); DSCR covenant with a cash-cure-only remedy (Issue 9); exit fee at scheduled maturity stacked on the prepayment fee (Issue 10); +5.00% default rate (Issue 11); sole-discretion property-manager consent (Issue 12); guarantor financial covenants with no cure period (Issue 13); Issue 13A on the guaranty's unilateral-modification clause (Guaranty §8.1); material adverse change event of default (Issue 14).<\/td><\/tr>\n    <tr><td>Medium (8)<\/td><td>Replacement reserve of $300 per unit against the borrower's $250 budget ($93,600 against $78,000 on 312 units, a $15,600 gap); missing ARRC-style SOFR fallback; insurance above market; environmental timing and update requirements; general indemnity and drafting errors (wrong ESA project number, guaranty cross-references, a 60-day versus 90-day involuntary-bankruptcy cure mismatch, and the model's \"No RECs identified\" entry, which conflicts with the ESA's single REC).<\/td><\/tr>\n  <\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p class=\"note\">Section XII of the memo lists provisions the agent reviewed but did not flag, consistent with the partner's instruction not to redline market terms. Section XIII sets out negotiation priorities in three tiers.<\/p>\n\n<figure>\n<svg data-pil-kind=\"plot\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 320\" width=\"640\" height=\"320\" role=\"img\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"dscr-title dscr-desc\">\n  <title id=\"dscr-title\">DSCR values from the underwriting model against 1.25x and 1.30x thresholds<\/title>\n  <desc id=\"dscr-desc\">Bar chart of five DSCR values, 1.20x, 1.29x, 1.34x, 1.17x and 1.22x, with dashed lines at 1.25x (covenant) and 1.30x (extension test). Bars below 1.25x are drawn in a warm colour.<\/desc>\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"260\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"260\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"260\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n  <text x=\"72\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">1.00x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"72\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">1.20x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"72\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">1.40x<\/text>\n  <rect x=\"102\" y=\"140\" width=\"60\" height=\"120\" fill=\"#b5543c\"/>\n  <rect x=\"206\" y=\"86\" width=\"60\" height=\"174\" fill=\"#4a6fa5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"310\" y=\"56\" width=\"60\" height=\"204\" fill=\"#4a6fa5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"414\" y=\"158\" width=\"60\" height=\"102\" fill=\"#b5543c\"/>\n  <rect x=\"518\" y=\"128\" width=\"60\" height=\"132\" fill=\"#b5543c\"/>\n  <text x=\"132\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\">1.20x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"236\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\">1.29x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\">1.34x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"444\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\">1.17x<\/text>\n  <text x=\"548\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\">1.22x<\/text>\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#222\">1.25x DSCR covenant<\/text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#222\">1.30x extension test<\/text>\n  <text x=\"132\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Col 1<\/text>\n  <text x=\"236\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Col 2<\/text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Col 3<\/text>\n  <text x=\"444\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Col 4<\/text>\n  <text x=\"548\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Col 5<\/text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">Warm bars: below the 1.25x covenant. Columns are in model order; the model's column labels are inconsistent.<\/text>\n<\/svg>\n<figcaption>Figure 1. The DSCR row of the borrower underwriting model (Sheet 2), in model order: 1.20x, 1.29x, 1.34x, 1.17x, 1.22x. Covenant status matches the model's covenant row (below in Columns 1, 4 and 5). The model's labels do not map cleanly to years, so the bars are shown by position only.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n\n<h2>4. Outcome<\/h2>\n<p>The record ends with the agent's final message. It reports that <span class=\"ref\">issue-memorandum.docx<\/span> was written to the agent's workspace root, with process notes in <span class=\"ref\">process/<\/span>. The record does not show that the memorandum was sent to Katherine Cheng, that any redline was prepared, or that anything was sent to Ironclad or Prescott Graves. The agent offered to prepare a redline and a cover letter as next steps; neither was produced in the record.<\/p>\n\n<h2>5. What the user should know<\/h2>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The extension conclusion is open.<\/strong> The agent's own reasoning (<span class=\"ref\">card_0077<\/span>) noted that the first extension DSCR test is probably measured on interest-only debt service. On that basis the Year 3 figure of about 1.345x would pass the 1.30x test. The Year 3 debt yield of 7.93% would still fail the 8.00% test. The memo's Issue 4 is titled as structurally unachievable and concludes that the effective term is three years. The records I read do not show that the first-extension point was reconciled in the final text, and that conclusion depends on it.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>A guaranty cross-reference was flagged but not confirmed as corrected.<\/strong> The first draft of Issue 13 cited \"Guaranty §6.1(d)\" for reporting. The agent noted (<span class=\"ref\">card_0073<\/span>) that §6.1 is the cooperation section and that the reporting obligation sits in §3.4, and it listed the fix. No later card shows the correction applied. The later checks confirmed that cited sections exist, which would not by itself catch a wrong subsection.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>Statute references are not sourced in the record.<\/strong> The memo refers to Georgia provisions (O.C.G.A. §10-7-3, material alteration; and §44-14-161, anti-deficiency) and describes certain terms as standard. The records contain no statute text for these citations, and the verification checked citations only against the loan documents and model. Verify them before relying on them.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>The loan agreement was read in pieces.<\/strong> Tool output was repeatedly truncated, so the agent re-read sections in ranges. The records do not show a single complete pass. Coverage of the full agreement therefore rests on those re-reads.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>The model has internal inconsistencies.<\/strong> The DSCR row has five values against six labelled columns, and the Returns tab labels exits as Years 3–5. The agent relied on the model's covenant-compliance block and its Returns-tab NOI. The memo quotes returns figures from the Returns tab (Year 3: 1.04x and 1.3%; Year 5: 1.37x and 8.4%; 8.1% and 1.36x at the lender-reserve level). The Year 3 profit figure of $705,499 does not reconcile with the total-return and equity figures shown on the same tab, so those returns figures carry the same uncertainty.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>One possible issue was not checked.<\/strong> The records do not show whether the 18-month prepayment lockout (LA §2.5) would make a mandatory casualty or condemnation prepayment during the lockout an Event of Default. The records I read do not show this point being checked.<\/li>\n  <li><strong>\"Verified\" means checked against extracted text.<\/strong> The first scripted check reported 15 failures out of 265. The agent attributed them to formatting or checker error. The record does not confirm that the origination-fee dollar amount ($357,000) was added to the memo, although the agent said it would consider this (<span class=\"ref\">card_0107<\/span>); the memo states the fee as a percentage. The process note's severity header reads \"High 10\" but lists 11 issues; the final count of 4 Critical, 11 High and 8 Medium matches the list. None of this is a legal review by a lawyer.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<\/body>\n<\/html>\n"}, "same_run": "harvey-labs--codex--real-estate-identify-issues-in-counterpartys-proposed-commercial-real-estate-loan-agreement"});
